{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1uemZmpp9uo9GatEC1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HHwrMujL3AvggbtdG1a85o","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-070556__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":360,"limit":540},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13320,"cache_read_input_tokens":25675,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13320},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"c31afd62-e15a-44af-9fde-afa2cb658632","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:06:14.714Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1ueXgAu1b9baE7ZUe2","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HHwrMujL3AvggbtdG1a85o":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-070556__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":360,"limit":540}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHwrMujL3AvggbtdG1a85o","type":"tool_result","content":"360\t**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players\n361\t(about 1.9–2.2 yd tall at rig sizes 1.2–1.45) and the store's props stay in proportion. z is always up. Angles are\n362\tradians, 0 east. `FF.u.yd(n) = n * 16`.\n363\t\n364\t### 5.2 Field geometry\n365\t- The playing field is 100 yards between the goal lines, plus 10-yard end zones, so 120 × 53 yards.\n366\t- The TileMap is 128 columns × 70 rows. Columns 0–3 are outside the left end, 4–123 are the 120 field yards (end\n367\t  zones at 4–13 and 114–123), and 124–127 are outside the right end.\n368\t- Rows from the top: 0–7 are the far stands (8 tiers), 8–11 the far sideline area, 12–64 the 53 field rows, and 65–69\n369\t  the near sideline (benches, cheerleaders, photographers, chain gang).\n370\t- World constants (in `25-field.js`): `END_L = 64` (the left end line, x), `GOAL_L = 224`, `GOAL_R = 1824`,\n371\t  `END_R = 1984`, `SIDE_T = 192` (the far sideline, y), `SIDE_B = 1040` (the near sideline, y), `MID_Y = 616`.\n372\t- Yard lines run every 5 yards. Numbers every 10 sit 12 yards in from each sideline. Hash marks every yard sit at\n373\t  `MID_Y ± 3 yd` (arcade hashes, close to the middle). A team abbreviation is painted in each end zone, and the home\n374\t  logo at midfield.\n375\t- The ball spot after a play: x where the ball was when it died, y clamped to the hashes.\n376\t- A team attacking right has `dir = +1`; attacking left, `dir = -1`. Field position \"own 20\" for a team attacking right\n377\t  is x = `GOAL_L + 20*16`. `FF.sim.yardline(x, side)` converts both ways.\n378\t- Goalposts sit at each end line (x = END_L, END_R; y = MID_Y): a base post, a gooseneck 1 yd toward the field, the\n379\t  crossbar at **53** units (10 ft), and uprights **99** units apart (18.5 ft) rising **160** above the bar. Draw them\n380\t  with `r.queue` lines like the SPORTS kit's posts, 2 px wide, in yellow `#ffd84a` with a `#c89a1a` shaded side. They\n381\t  are drawn, not shapes, because they need exact proportions and a hit test for kicks.\n382\t- Pylons sit at the 4 goal-line and 4 end-line corners (shape `pylon`, §12.6).\n383\t\n384\t### 5.3 The TileMap\n385\t- Legend: `.` grass (floor tag `grass`), `e` end zone (`endzone`), `s` sideline (`sideline`), `m` mud (`mud`), `i` ice\n386\t  (`ice`), `,` outside (`outside`), and `1`–`8` the stand tiers (wall types 11–18).\n387\t- Wall types 11–18: heights 14, 26, 38, 50, 62, 74, 86, 98, each 1 tile deep, with\n388\t  `cut: true, cutH: 8` so reversed and rotated views see past them. Faces and roofs come from the stadium\n389\t  (`face: 'plank'` bleachers at Tumbleweed, `'stone'` concrete at the Mega Dome, `roof: 'slab'`).\n390\t- Tiers rise away from the field. Row 7, the front row next to the far sideline area, is type 11 (h 14), and each row\n391\t  further back is one type higher, up to row 0 = type 18 (h 98). A stadium with N tiers (§18.2: 3 to 8) fills rows\n392\t  8−N to 7. The row behind its top tier (7−N) is the back wall, and any rows above that are `outside`.\n393\t- Behind the top tier is a back wall (type 19, h 120) with banners (props) and, by stadium, a scoreboard or jumbotron\n394\t  shape.\n395\t- Side stands behind each end zone (columns 0–3) are 4 tiers (types 11–14), so an end zone view has a crowd too.\n396\t- `outside`: the stadium's ground color beyond the map. The sky comes from an `E.Backdrop` drawn first in `draw`\n397\t  (mesas, bayou trees, a skyline, mountains with the northern lights, the dome's ceiling truss). It shows above the\n398\t  far stands when the camera looks high.\n399\t\n400\t### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n401\t- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n402\t  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n403\t- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n404\t  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n405\t  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n406\t- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n407\t  runtime (§11.5).\n408\t- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n409\t  between the hashes.\n410\t- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n411\t  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n412\t\n413\t### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\n414\tEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\n415\tcooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\n416\tmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\n417\tbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\n418\tprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n419\t\n420\t### 5.6 Views\n421\t| Id | Made as | Used for |\n422\t|---|---|---|\n423\t| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n424\t| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n425\t| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n426\t| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n427\t| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n428\t| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n429\t| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n430\t\n431\tSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\n432\tthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n433\t\n434\t---\n435\t\n436\t## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n437\t\n438\t### 6.1 The match state machine\n439\t```\n440\tCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\n441\tDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n442\t               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n443\t               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n444\t               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n445\t```\n446\t- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n447\t  scenario's. The winner receives.\n448\t- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n449\t  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n450\t  special cards.\n451\t- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n452\t  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n453\t  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n454\t- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n455\t  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n456\t  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n457\t- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n458\t  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n459\t- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n460\t  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n461\t- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n462\t- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n463\t- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n464\t  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n465\t- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n466\t  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n467\t  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n468\t- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n469\t\n470\t### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n471\t- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n472\t  on downs at the spot.\n473\t- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n474\t- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n475\t- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n476\t- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n477\t- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n478\t\n479\t### 6.3 Scoring\n480\t| Score | Points | After |\n481\t|---|---|---|\n482\t| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n483\t| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n484\t| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n485\t| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n486\t| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n487\t| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n488\t\n489\t### 6.4 Kicks\n490\t- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n491\t  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n492\t- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n493\t  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n494\t  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n495\t- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n496\t  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n497\t- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n498\t  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n499\t\n500\t### 6.5 The clock\n501\t- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n502\t  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n503\t- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n504\t  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n505\t- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n506\t- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n507\t- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n508\t  dead.\n509\t\n510\t### 6.6 Turnovers and possession changes\n511\t- **Interception:** the defender has the ball live and can return it. Control flips (§9.4).\n512\t- **Fumble:** the ball goes loose (§7.7). Whoever recovers has possession. A recovery by the defense is live (can be\n513\t  returned). A recovery by the offense is live too (arcade).\n514\t- **Lateral or backward pass:** legal any time. A bad lateral that hits the ground is a loose ball (a fumble).\n515\t- **Turnover on downs:** the other side takes the ball at the spot.\n516\t- After a change of possession, the line of scrimmage, `dir` and the down reset for the new offense.\n517\t\n518\t---\n519\t\n520\t## 7. Athletes, movement and actions\n521\t\n522\t### 7.1 Ratings\n523\tEach athlete has ratings 1–10: `SPD` speed, `ACC` acceleration and agility (cuts, spins), `STR` strength (blocking,\n524\tbreaking tackles), `HND` hands (catching, ball security), `TCK` tackling, `ARM` throw power, `AIM` throw accuracy,\n525\t`AWR` awareness (AI reaction and reads), `KCK` kicking (power and steadiness). Team ratings on the team-select screen\n526\tare 4 bars averaged from the roster: PASS, RUN, DEFENSE, SPECIAL.\n527\t\n528\t### 7.2 Movement\n529\t- Top speed in units/s = `T.speedBase` (92) + SPD × `T.speedPerSpd` (7), so SPD 2 is 106 (about 6.6 yd/s, a lineman)\n530\t  and SPD 10 is 162 (10.1 yd/s, Zap Zapata).\n531\t- Carrying the ball costs −4%. Backpedaling (moving against `facing` by more than 100°) costs −35%.\n532\t- Acceleration = `T.accelBase` (520) + ACC × 30 units/s². Braking is 900. Turn rate is 9 rad/s at speed and 16 rad/s\n533\t  slow (`E.approachAng`).\n534\t- **Turbo** (the `turbo` action, held): × `T.turboMult` (1.25) speed and × 1.3 acceleration. It drains `T.turboDrain`\n535\t  (0.45/s of a full bar) and regenerates `T.turboRegen` (0.22/s) when not held. Each dead ball refills +35%.\n536\t  Big Hits need turbo ≥ 0.15 and cost 0.15. Every athlete has a bar; the HUD shows the bars of human-held athletes.\n537\t- Surfaces: `mud` × 0.85 speed and × 0.7 acceleration, dives slide 1.5× farther. `ice` × 0.7 grip: velocity\n538\t  approaches the desired one at half the rate (slides), and cuts are wider. Snow weather × 0.95 speed. These come from\n539\t  the floor tag under the athlete (`map.floorAt`).\n540\t- Bodies are `E.Body` (r 6; linemen r 7.5; gravity 700) with `body.update(dt, map)` for z, jumps and the stands.\n541\t  The sim sets `vx, vy` from intents each step.\n542\t\n543\t### 7.3 Collisions between athletes\n544\t- Circle against circle every step for every pair (91 pairs at 14 athletes; no hash needed). Separate along the\n545\t  normal in proportion to the inverse of mass (mass = 1 + STR × 0.15; linemen get +0.6).\n546\t- Teammates push gently. Opponents push hard: no overlap.\n547\t- An overlap with the ball carrier during LIVE is a **contact** and may start a tackle (§7.5). An overlap between a\n548\t  blocker and a rusher starts an **engagement** (§7.9).\n549\t- Refs and sideline cast don't collide with the sim. Refs are pushed aside cosmetically and fall comically if a play\n550\t  runs through them (§10.8).\n551\t\n552\t### 7.4 The action system\n553\tEach athlete has at most one action at a time: `{ id, t, dur, phase, data }`. Actions lock movement as their table\n554\tsays, set the athlete's **pose code** (Appendix C) for the presentation, and resolve at their key moment.\n555\t\n556\t| Action | Who | Input | Duration | Key moment and effect |\n557\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n558\t| `hike` | C | the offense's b1 in PRESNAP | 0.25 s | the ball to the QB (shotgun: an arc in 0.3 s; under center: in hand) |\n559\t| `handoff` | QB → RB | automatic on run plays at the play's `handoffT` | 0.35 s | the ball changes hands when within 1.2 yd; a pitch or toss is a short arc |\n560\t| `throw` | QB | a receiver button, or a click on a receiver | 0.28 s wind + release | the ball's flight (§7.6); with turbo held, a Rocket Pass |\n561\t| `dive` | carrier | b1 | 0.45 s air + 0.6 s down | a lunge of 2.5 yd at 1.4× speed; the ball spot is where he lands; the play ends (down) unless he's in the end zone, which is a TD |\n562\t| `spin` | carrier | b3 | 0.32 s | evade window 0.0–0.22 s: a contact from a tackler that isn't behind him (angle > 110° from his facing) misses; speed × 0.8 during the spin; cooldown 0.8 s |\n563\t| `stiffArm` | carrier | b2 | 0.4 s | a tackler in front (within 60° of facing, within 1.8 yd) gets the break chance + `T.stiffBonus` (0.35); on a break the tackler stumbles (0.6 s down) |\n564\t| `hurdle` | carrier | b4 | 0.55 s (`body.jump(260)`) | airborne: low dives (`dive`, `tackleDive`) pass under him; a standing tackler isn't evaded |\n565\t| `lateral` | carrier | lb or rb | 0.25 s | pitch to the nearest teammate behind or beside him (within 100° of the backward direction, ≤ 9 yd), a 0.35–0.6 s arc; a bad lateral (no teammate in range) is thrown to the nearest spot and is a loose ball |\n566\t| `tackleDive` | defender | b1 (the AI decides when, §8.4) | 0.45 s lunge + 0.7 s down on a miss | contact with the carrier during the lunge resolves a tackle (§7.5) |\n567\t| `bigHit` | defender | turbo + b1 | 0.35 s lunge at 1.6× speed | as a tackle with `T.bigHitBonus` (+0.12) and a fumble roll × 3; a miss is 1.0 s down |\n568\t| `strip` | defender | b3 | 0.3 s | within 1.2 yd of the carrier: fumble chance `T.stripChance` (0.12) − HND × 0.01; otherwise a whiff |\n569\t| `jump` | any | b4 | 0.5 s | a jump for a catch, an INT or a swat: +16 units reach height and +6 catch radius for the ball arriving during 0.12–0.42 s |\n570\t| `diveCatch` | receiver | b1 while the ball is in the air | 0.45 s | +10 catch radius for low balls (arrival z < 12); he ends on the ground |\n571\t| `kick` | K or P | the meter (§7.8) | 0.6 s | the ball's flight from the meter's power and accuracy |\n572\t| `lateHit` | anyone | b1 within `T.lateHitWindow` after the whistle, an opponent within 2 yd | 0.5 s | cosmetic knockdown, crowd boos and cheers, the announcer's outrage, the \"Late hits\" stat |\n573\t| `celebrate` | scorer | auto or b2/b3/b4 | 3.5 s | dance (§10.6) |\n574\t| `getUp` | anyone downed | auto | 0.6–1.0 s | back on his feet |\n575\t\n576\tInputs are buffered for 0.15 s (`input.buffered` + `consume`) so a press slightly early still happens.\n577\t\n578\t### 7.5 Tackle resolution\n579\tWhen a tackler's lunge, or an engaged contact, touches the carrier (centers within r1 + r2 + 4):\n580\t```\n581\tp = T.tackleBase (0.62) + 0.03*TCK(t) - 0.015*STR(c) - 0.012*ACC(c)\n582\t  + (fromBehind ? 0.15 : 0) + (bigHit ? T.bigHitBonus : 0) + (carrier diving or hurdling ? +0.25 : 0)\n583\t  + bus.mod('tackle.p', ctx)    // cards, Hot Streak, Dinged, specials, difficulty\n584\tp = clamp(p, 0.15, 0.97)\n585\t```\n586\t- Evasions are checked first: a spin's window, a stiff arm's frontal break, a hurdle against a low dive.\n587\t- Success: the carrier goes down (pose `TACKLED`, falling the way the tackle pushed him), the ball is dead where it\n588\t  is, and the tackler lands on him. On a **Big Hit**, the carrier is launched along the hit direction at\n589\t  `T.bigHitLaunch` (180 u/s) and up at 150 u/s (pose `FLY`). Hit-stop 0.08 s, shake 4, a comic \"BOOM!\" (§11.3).\n590\t  The fumble roll is `T.fumbleBase` (0.03) × (bigHit 3) × (rain or snow 1.6) × (1 − HND × 0.05) + mods. A fumbled\n591\t  ball comes loose at the moment of the hit (§7.7).\n592\t- Failure: the tackler's lunge carries on and he lands (0.7 s down; 1.0 after a Big Hit). The carrier loses 25% of\n593\t  his speed for 0.3 s (a stumble) and gets a \"BROKEN TACKLE!\" pop when he was going ≥ 60% of top speed.\n594\t- **Gang tackles:** 2+ tacklers in contact within 0.3 s get +0.18 each beyond the first.\n595\t\n596\t### 7.6 The ball (`28-ball.js`)\n597\tStates: `held` (by an athlete), `snap`, `pass`, `kick`, `loose`, `dead`.\n598\t- **Held:** at the holder's carry point (sim: 6 units in front of the hip at z 14). The presentation draws it at the\n599\t  rig's hand through the gear hook (§12.2).\n600\t- **Pass:** analytic flight. At the throw, pick the target point P = the receiver's predicted position at the arrival\n601\t  time (iterate twice: t = d / speed). Speed by type: lob `T.lobSpeed` (240) + ARM × 8 u/s along the ground, bullet\n602\t  (Rocket Pass) `T.bulletSpeed` (400) + ARM × 10. Height at release is 30, and the ball aims to arrive at chest\n603\t  height (z 20): `E.arc({ x, y, z: 30 }, { x: P.x, y: P.y, z: 20 }, T.passGravity (300), { time: t })` gives the\n604\t  velocity. Passes use their own gravity, 300 (loose balls use 600), so the arcs read well. A 30-yd bullet (about\n605\t  1.2 s) peaks about 3 yd up, and a lob (about 1.6 s) about 6 yd up, a rainbow over the defender.\n606\t  - **Accuracy:** P gets a gaussian offset with σ = `T.aimError` (0.035) × distance × (1.3 − AIM × 0.06) × (pressure:\n607\t    a rusher within 2 yd 1.6; throwing on the run 1.25) + mods.\n608\t  - **Max range:** `T.maxPass` (55 yd) + ARM × 1.5 yd. Beyond it the ball is short.\n609\t  - **In flight:** the ball spins (draw the spiral, §12.7). A marker shows the landing point on the ground for the\n610\t    receiving team's human and the defending human (an X in team color, with a ring that shrinks as it nears).\n611\t  - **Arrival** (the ball at z ≤ 34 near P): resolve the catch (§7.7). If no one is in range, or the ball hits the\n612\t    ground, the pass is incomplete (a bounce: a short random-direction `loose` bounce for 0.6 s, cosmetic, dead).\n613\t- **Kick:** analytic flight to the landing point the meter gives, by `E.arc(from, landing, T.kickGravity (260),\n614\t  { apex })`, with apex `T.kickApex` (punts 260, kickoffs 220, FGs and PATs 150, squibs 20, onside 60). The hang time\n615\t  follows: a punt is about 2.8 s. A high kick may leave the top of the screen. The KICK shot zooms out to 0.75, and\n616\t  the shadow and landing marker keep the ball readable. On landing it goes `loose`: the `E.Body` ball, bounce 0.45,\n617\t  with friction. A football bounces erratically, so on each bounce add a random ±25° turn and ±30% speed from\n618\t  `FF.rng.sim`.\n619\t- **Loose:** an `E.Body` (r 3, bounce 0.45, gravity 600) with erratic bounces as above. Players dive at it (§7.7).\n620\t- Out of bounds (y outside the sidelines, or past an end line) kills the ball at the crossing point. A kick out of\n621\t  bounds follows §6.4.\n622\t\n623\t### 7.7 Catches, interceptions, deflections, fumbles and pile-ups\n624\t**Catch resolution** at arrival. Candidates are athletes whose reach covers the ball. The horizontal distance must be\n625\t≤ `T.catchRadius` (14) (+6 jumping in window, +10 diving), and the arrival height must be ≤ reach (34 standing, 50\n626\tjumping). For each candidate:\n627\t```\n628\toffense: pc = T.catchBase (0.66) + 0.03*HND - 0.22*(defenders within 14) - (bullet ? 0.10 : 0)\n629\t             + (jump in window ? 0.08 : 0) + bus.mod('catch.p')        // Glue Gloves, Butterfingers, Hot Streak\n630\tdefense: pi = 0.10 + 0.035*HND + (jump in window ? 0.12 : 0) + (in front of receiver ? 0.10 : 0) + bus.mod('int.p')\n631\t```\n632\t- Order the candidates by distance to the ball. Each nearest defender rolls first: INT with `pi`, else a\n633\t  **deflection** with 0.35 (the ball pops up to z 20–40 with a random velocity and can be caught again).\n634\t- Then the receiver rolls `pc`: a catch, or a **drop** (\"DROPPED!\", the ball bounces dead).\n635\t- Cheats and specials can force outcomes: `forceCatch`, `forceINT`, Golden Spiral (no INT).\n636\t- A catch gives the receiver the ball live with his velocity kept (catch and run). Control follows (§9.4).\n637\t\n638\t**Fumbles and loose balls.** The ball pops out with a velocity away from the hit plus up 60–110 u/s. Every athlete\n639\twithin `T.pileRadius` (5 yd) of the ball gets the brain behavior `recover` (dive at the ball). Humans dive with b1.\n640\t- The first athlete whose dive or feet touch the ball while z ≤ 6 gets a **recovery**. Rolled, not instant: if 2+\n641\t  athletes are within 1.5 yd at that moment, a **pile-up** starts.\n642\t- **Pile-up** (1.6 s): every athlete within 2.5 yd of the ball dives in (poses `PILE`, stacked by z offsets), a dust\n643\t  or mud cloud, the camera punches in, the crowd \"OOOH\". Then the ref \"digs\" (comic) and signals possession, a roll\n644\t  weighted by each piler's distance to the ball when it started (closer is more likely) and STR. \"WHO'S GOT IT?! ...\n645\t  THE FIASCOS HAVE IT!\"\n646\t- A loose ball that goes out of bounds belongs to the team that last had it.\n647\t\n648\t### 7.8 Kicking and the kick meter\n649\tA 3-press meter in screen space near the kicker (§14.7):\n650\t1. b1 starts the needle sweeping a power bar. It rises at `T.meterSpeed` (1.4 sweeps/s), then falls back.\n651\t2. b1 again sets the **power** (0–1). Past 0.95 is the red zone: +5% distance but the accuracy needle runs 1.5×\n652\t   faster.\n653\t3. The accuracy needle sweeps across a center band. b1 sets **accuracy** (−1..1). |acc| ≤ the band\n654\t   (`T.accBand` 0.12; Super Foot ×2.5) is straight; otherwise the kick hooks by acc × 9° (FG) or acc × 14° (punt\n655\t   and kickoff).\n656\t- Before step 1 the stick aims: ±15° for punts and kickoffs, ±4° for FGs (the Kick Cam shows the aim line).\n657\t- Distance: FG = 25 + power × (25 + KCK × 1.6) yd, so KCK 7 at full power is about 61 yd, before wind. Punt: 30–55\n658\t  yd. Kickoff: as §6.4.\n659\t- The FG test: when the ball reaches the end line's x, it's good if y is between the uprights (±49.5) and z > 53.\n660\t  A ball off an upright (within 3 units) bounces \"DOINK!\" and is no good (or good if it falls inside: roll it).\n661\t- CPU kickers: power and accuracy from KCK and difficulty, plus gaussian noise (σ 0.08 / 0.10).\n662\t- A mouse left click, b1 and Space all press the meter.\n663\t\n664\t### 7.9 Blocking\n665\t- **Engagement:** a blocker and a rusher overlap, or come within 1.5 yd with the blocker facing him, which locks them\n666\t  as a pair: they hold 0.8 yd apart, facing each other.\n667\t- **Contest:** each step the pair moves together with velocity `T.blockPush` (18 u/s) × (STR_r − STR_b +\n668\t  rushMoveBonus + (turbo ? 2 : 0)) toward the rusher's goal (the QB or the carrier) when positive, or the blocker's\n669\t  drive direction when negative. Run blocks add the blocker's drive (+1.5).\n670\t- **Shed:** the rusher sheds when his shed timer runs out. `shedTime = T.shedBase (1.9 s) × (STR_b / STR_r) ×\n671\t  rand(0.7, 1.3)`, or ∞ under Brick Wall. On a shed the rusher gets a 0.3 s burst (×1.2) toward his target, and the\n672\t  blocker staggers 0.3 s. A human rusher can mash b3 (□) to cut his timer by 0.12 s per press (a swim move; max 6).\n673\t- **Pancake:** when STR_b − STR_r ≥ 3, a 15% roll on engagement knocks the rusher down for 1.2 s. \"PANCAKE!\"\n674\t- Pass blocks mirror the most dangerous unblocked rusher in the blocker's zone (left, middle, right). Run blocks\n675\t  follow the play's assignment (§8.2).\n676\t- A human-controlled lineman (co-op, or by switching) moves freely. Engagement then happens on contact.\n677\t\n678\t---\n679\t\n680\t## 8. The AI (`33-ai.js`, `34-ai-coach.js`)\n681\t\n682\t### 8.1 Brains and behaviors\n683\tEvery athlete not held by a human (or by a remote player) has a brain. A brain picks a **behavior** from its play\n684\tassignment and the phase, re-thinks at 20 Hz (staggered: `(step + index) % 6 === 0`), and every step turns the current\n685\tbehavior into an intent (a move vector, turbo, presses). Reaction delays come from `AWR` and difficulty: an AI sees a\n686\t**delayed** world. It reads other athletes' positions from a 0.1–0.4 s history buffer, which makes cutbacks, jukes\n687\tand play fakes work against it.\n688\t\n689\t| Behavior | Role | What it does |\n690\t|---|---|---|\n691\t| `formation` | all, PRESNAP | jog to the formation spot, face the line, take the stance |\n692\t| `passBlock(zone)` | OL, RB on some plays | hold a spot 1.5–2.5 yd in front of the QB in his zone, mirror the most dangerous rusher there, engage |\n693\t| `runBlock(target, drive)` | OL, WR, TE | engage the assigned defender and drive him along `drive`, or climb to the 2nd level |\n694\t| `pull(path, then)` | G on sweeps and counters | run the path, then lead-block the first defender in front of the carrier |\n695\t| `route(points, after)` | WR, RB | run the waypoints (cut sharply: brake to 60% at a break, 0.12 s), then `after`: `settle` (find the nearest hole in the zones, face the QB), `scramble` (work toward the sideline the QB scrambles to) or `block` |\n696\t| `carry(path)` | the carrier | follow the play's path while it's open, then **free running**: score 16 directions each 0.1 s, `forward·w1 − Σ threat(defender) − sideline penalty − crowding`, where threat = closing speed / distance² for defenders within 8 yd; pick the best with hysteresis; turbo when no defender is within 4 yd or one is closing from behind; evasive moves, below |\n697\t| `qbPass(drop, reads)` | QB | drop back to the play's spot. From `readT` (0.6–1.0 s) read the receivers in order. **Openness** = the distance from the nearest defender to the receiver's arrival point, minus 8 per defender within 3 yd of the pass lane. Throw to the first receiver with openness > `T.aiOpen` (3 yd; by difficulty) whose route is ≥ 40% done. At 2.8 s, or with a rusher within 2 yd, throw to the best, scramble if the best is < 1 yd open, or throw it away out of bounds (Pro and Fiasco difficulty). Scramble: `carry`, and still look to throw while behind the line. |\n698\t| `qbRun` | QB on sneaks and the run fakes of draws | his part of the play script |\n699\t| `rush(gap)` | DL, blitzers | attack the gap toward the QB, engage blockers, try to shed; once free, `pursue` the QB; when the QB throws, `pursue` the ball |\n700\t| `manCover(target)` | DB, LB | keep a cushion of `T.cushion` (2 yd, smaller near the goal line) on the goal side of the target, matching his speed with the reaction delay; when the ball is in the air, break to the arrival point after the reaction delay and jump when within 2 yd as it arrives |\n701\t| `zoneCover(zone)` | DB, LB | drop to the zone center; pick the most threatening receiver inside (zone radius + 3 yd), weighted by depth and how open he is; shadow him; release him when he leaves; the ball in the air is as above |\n702\t| `spy(QB)` | LB | mirror the QB at 5 yd; when the QB crosses the line, `pursue` |\n703\t| `pursue(carrier)` | all defenders, once there is a carrier past the line, a scramble, or a return | head for the intercept point: the smallest t with `|c + v_c·t − d| ≤ speed_d·t`, solved in 0.05 s steps up to 3 s, with the angle limited by the sideline. Tackle decision below |\n704\t| `kickCover(lane)` | the kicking team | sprint downfield in lanes 6 yd apart, then `pursue` the returner |\n705\t| `returnBlock` | the receiving team | form a wedge of 3 in front of the returner's lane; engage the nearest coverage man |\n706\t| `recover` | everyone near a loose ball | dive at the ball (§7.7) |\n707\t| `celebrate`, `dejected`, `idle` | after the play | poses (§10.6) |\n708\t\n709\t**Evasive moves for an AI carrier.** When a tackler is within 1.6 yd and closing: against a diving tackler, hurdle\n710\twith chance 0.25 + ACC × 0.03; against a frontal one, stiff arm with 0.2 + STR × 0.03; against one from the side,\n711\tspin with 0.2 + ACC × 0.03. One move per 0.8 s. Difficulty scales these.\n712\t\n713\t**The tackle decision (AI tacklers).** With a reachable carrier, dive when the distance is under 1.8 yd and the angle\n714\tto his predicted position is under 35°. Use a Big Hit when turbo ≥ 0.15 and a roll of 0.2 + TCK × 0.03 passes (×1.5\n715\twhen Hot). Otherwise keep pursuing to contact (a wrap-up on contact counts as a tackle attempt with −0.08).\n716\t\n717\t### 8.2 Play assignments (data to brain)\n718\t`FF.data.plays[id].assign[slot]` (Appendix A.2) is read when PRESNAP ends:\n719\t- Offense: `{ block: 'pass' | 'run', zone, target, drive }`, `{ pull: [[x, y], ...], then: 'lead' }`,\n720\t  `{ route: [[x, y], ...], after: 'settle', read: n }`, `{ carry: [[x, y], ...], handoffT }`, and the QB's\n721\t  `{ drop: [x, y], reads: ['WR2', 'WR1', 'RB'], readT, fake }`.\n722\t- Defense: `{ rush: 'A' | 'B' | 'C' | 'contain' }`, `{ man: 'WR1' | 'WR2' | 'RB' | 'C' }`,\n723\t  `{ zone: { at: [x, y], r } }`, `{ spy: 'QB' }`.\n724\t- Play coordinates are in **yards** from the ball spot: x downfield (+ is the offense's attacking direction), y\n725\t  across (+ is the offense's right). Mirroring (FLIP) negates y. Defense coordinates use the same frame (x is the\n726\t  distance past the line toward the defense's own goal). The sim converts with `dir`.\n727\t- Targets in defense assignments point at offensive slots. If the offense's formation lacks a slot, fall back to\n728\t  zone `[8, 0], r 7`.\n729\t\n730\t### 8.3 Special teams brains\n731\tKick coverage, wedge returns, the punt shield (3 blockers in front of the punter for 1.5 s), FG protection\n732\t(pass-block in place) and the FG rush (all-out `rush`; 2 jumpers in the middle `jump` at the kick).\n733\t\n734\t### 8.4 Difficulty\n735\t| | Rookie | Pro | Fiasco |\n736\t|---|---|---|---|\n737\t| CPU reaction delay | 0.38 s | 0.24 s | 0.13 s |\n738\t| CPU tackle p × | 0.85 | 1.0 | 1.12 |\n739\t| CPU QB openness threshold | 4.5 yd | 3 yd | 2 yd |\n740\t| CPU kick noise × | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.6 |\n741\t| CPU coverage cushion × | 1.3 | 1.0 | 0.85 |\n742\t| Catch-up assist (`T.rubberBand`): when the human trails by ≥ 14, his team gets +0.05 catch, +0.04 tackle and −0.04 to the CPU's | on | off | off |\n743\tStory chapters set a difficulty per chapter (§15.1), scaled by the Difficulty setting.\n744\t\n745\t### 8.5 The coach AI (play calling, cards, decisions)\n746\t- **Situation features:** down, distance, field position, score difference, time left, quarter, and the opponent's\n747\t  last 5 play types (Fiasco difficulty only).\n748\t- **Offense:** start from the team's tendency weights (`ai.run`, `ai.pass`, `ai.trick`, §18.1), then apply the\n749\t  situation rules in order:\n750\t  - 3rd/4th & 10+ means pass deep or mid (×2.5).\n751\t  - Less than 3 to go means run, sneak or quick passes (×2).\n752\t  - Goal to go inside the 5 means GOAL LINE runs, slants and fades.\n753\t  - In the last 1:00 while trailing: sideline routes (Quick Outs, Out & Up) ×3, and get out of bounds.\n754\t  - Leading by 9+ in the last 2:00: runs and kneels.\n755\t  - 4th down: field goal if the kick is ≤ `T.cpuFgRange` (45 yd) + KCK. Go for it if the distance is ≤ 3 and the ball\n756\t    is past midfield, or if trailing late. Punt otherwise. A 5% fake punt roll on 4th & ≤ 5.\n757\t- **Defense:** counter the offense's tendency (blitz on 3rd & long ×1.8, goal line inside the 3, prevent while\n758\t  leading late), with 20% randomness so the CPU can be read only roughly.\n759\t- **Cards:** the CPU uses a card when its trigger matches (§10.2) and it holds one. At most one card per 3 plays.\n760\t- **PAT:** go for 2 when down by 2, 5, 9, 10 or 12 after the TD in the second half. Otherwise kick.\n761\t- **COACH SAYS:** run the offense logic for the human's side, and mark the top pick with a star badge on the\n762\t  play-call screen. If the human doesn't choose, it plays when the timer runs out.\n763\t\n764\t### 8.6 The Play Lab (an AI quality tool and test)\n765\t`FF.dev.playLab(offPlay, defPlay, n, { seed, teams, difficulty })` runs n plays headless and fast (presentation off)\n766\tfrom midfield on 1st & 20, both sides CPU. It returns `{ avgYards, completion, sacks, ints, fumbles, tds, avgTime,\n767\tmaxTime, stuck }`, where `stuck` counts plays over 25 s or an athlete idle more than 3 s while LIVE.\n768\t**The bands it must stay in** (every offensive play against each of the 8 non-kick defenses, n = 30, Pro, Fiascos vs\n769\tMudhogs):\n770\t- Nothing is `stuck`, and every play ends within 25 s.\n771\t- A pass play's completion, averaged over the 8 defenses, is 35–85% (HAIL MARY 5–40%), and its sack rate is under\n772\t  25%.\n773\t- A run play averages 1.5–9 yd over the 8 defenses.\n774\t- No play averages over 25 yd over the 8 defenses (no unstoppable play), and no defense holds every play under 1 yd\n775\t  (no unbeatable defense).\n776\tIn the browser the Dev menu runs the same lab on screen with debug overlays (§17).\n777\t\n778\t---\n779\t\n780\t## 9. Controls and human control (`35-control.js`)\n781\t\n782\t### 9.1 The input map (`FF.KEYS`)\n783\tA custom preset (scene `input: FF.KEYS`). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives a shared-keyboard half its 6\n784\tbuttons in this order. The engine's standard `confirm`/`cancel` are redefined so J and K don't mean menu\n785\tconfirm/cancel.\n786\t```js\n787\tFF.KEYS = {\n788\t  up: E.Input.DEFAULT.up, down: E.Input.DEFAULT.down, left: E.Input.DEFAULT.left, right: E.Input.DEFAULT.right,\n789\t  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n790\t  confirm: ['Enter', 'Space', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1'],\n791\t  b1: ['Space', 'KeyK', 'Pad0'],   // ✕ / A    hike, dive, tackle, kick meter, pick\n792\t  b2: ['KeyL', 'Pad1'],            // ○ / B    stiff arm, switch player, back\n793\t  b3: ['KeyJ', 'Pad2'],            // □ / X    spin, strip, flip play\n794\t  b4: ['KeyI', 'Pad3'],            // △ / Y    hurdle, jump, catch, Fiasco Card\n795\t  turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7'],   // R2 / RT\n796\t  lb: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],            // L1 / LB  lateral, previous tab\n797\t  rb: ['KeyE', 'Pad5'],            // R1 / RB  lateral, next tab\n798\t  r1: ['Digit1'], r2: ['Digit2'], r3: ['Digit3'], r4: ['Digit4']   // keyboard: throw to receiver 1-4\n799\t};\n800\t```\n801\tCheck `E.Input.DEFAULT` for the exact move codes (WASD, the arrows, the d-pad, `PadAxis*`). The Controls screen names\n802\tthem with `menu.controls: { b1: 'Hike · Dive · Tackle · Kick', b2: 'Stiff arm · Switch player', b3: 'Spin · Strip ·\n803\tFlip play', b4: 'Hurdle · Jump · Catch · Card', turbo: 'Turbo', lb: 'Lateral · Tab left', rb: 'Lateral · Tab right',\n804\tr1: 'Throw to receiver 1', ... }`.\n805\t\n806\t### 9.2 The control table by context\n807\t| Context | Action | PlayStation | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse |\n808\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n809\t| Menus | move the focus | d-pad / left stick | d-pad / left stick | arrows / WASD | hover moves the focus |\n810\t| | select | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | left click |\n811\t| | back | ○ | B | Esc / Backspace | the on-screen BACK button |\n812\t| | tabs | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | click a tab |\n813\t| Play call | pick the play | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | click a card |\n814\t| | flip it | □ | X | J | click FLIP |\n815\t| | Fiasco Card | △ (opens the card row) | Y | I | click a card slot |\n816\t| Pre-snap, offense | hike | ✕ | A | Space | click the QB |\n817\t| Pre-snap, defense | move your defender | stick | stick | WASD | — |\n818\t| | switch defender | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n819\t| QB behind the line | throw to receiver ✕ ○ □ △ | ✕ ○ □ △ | A B X Y | 1 2 3 4 (or K L J I) | click a receiver |\n820\t| | Rocket Pass | hold R2 + a receiver | RT + | Shift + | Shift + click |\n821\t| | scramble | stick (+ R2) | stick (+ RT) | WASD (+ Shift) | — |\n822\t| Ball carrier | turbo | R2 | RT | Shift | — |\n823\t| | dive | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n824\t| | stiff arm | ○ | B | L | — |\n825\t| | spin | □ | X | J | — |\n826\t| | hurdle | △ | Y | I | — |\n827\t| | lateral | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | — |\n828\t| Ball in the air, offense | jump catch | △ | Y | I | — |\n829\t| | diving catch | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n830\t| Defense, live | switch to the defender nearest the ball | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n831\t| | tackle (dive) | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n832\t| | Big Hit | R2 + ✕ | RT + A | Shift + Space | — |\n833\t| | jump, swat, intercept | △ | Y | I | — |\n834\t| | strip | □ | X | J | — |\n835\t| | swim move (while blocked) | □ repeatedly | X | J | — |\n836\t| After the whistle | late hit | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n837\t| Kicking | aim | stick ← → | stick | A / D, ← / → | — |\n838\t| | the meter (3 presses) | ✕ | A | Space | left click |\n839\t| Kick return in the end zone | take a knee | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n840\t| TD celebration | pick a dance | ○ □ △ | B X Y | L J I | — |\n841\t| Holding a boss star | his special (§10.5) | L1 + R1 together | LB + RB | Q + E | — |\n842\t| Anywhere | pause | Options | Menu | Esc / P | the corner menu button |\n843\t| Anywhere | change the camera view | — | — | V | — |\n844\tThe mouse wheel zooms the camera within the scene's range (engine standard, `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4] }`).\n845\t\n846\t### 9.3 Prompts that follow the device\n847\t- `FF.glyph.chip(g, x, y, action, { player, scale, device })` draws a button chip for that player's last device\n848\t  (`input.lastDevice`):\n849\t  - **Gamepad, PlayStation** (the default): a dark disc with the symbol drawn as pixels in its classic color: ✕ blue\n850\t    `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ae0a0`.\n851\t  - **Gamepad, Xbox** (Settings, Controller, Button symbols): colored discs with letters: A green, B red, X blue,\n852\t    Y yellow.\n853\t  - Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles labeled L1, R1, R2 or LB, RB, RT. Options/Menu are small pills.\n854\t  - **Keyboard:** a keycap (a light rectangle with a darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`. Wide\n855\t    caps for SPACE and SHIFT.\n856\t  - **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, used where the mouse does that context's job (clicking\n857\t    a receiver, a card, the kick meter). Elsewhere the keyboard chip.\n858\t  - Labels come from `input.label(action, device)` after rebinding. Never hard-code a key. Map the engine's label to\n859\t    a chip style: glyph labels (`✕ ○ □ △ A B X Y L1 ...`) are pad chips, everything else is keycaps.\n860\t- The receiver chips over receivers' heads show b1–b4 on a pad, r1–r4 (1–4) on a keyboard (b1–b4 on a shared-keyboard\n861\t  half), and a mouse cursor badge on the hovered receiver.\n862\t- **The prompt strip** (bottom right, 1–5 chips with words, §14.6) shows the current context's actions only: \"✕ HIKE\",\n863\t  then \"✕ ○ □ THROW · R2 ROCKET\", then \"□ SPIN ○ STIFF △ HURDLE ✕ DIVE R2 TURBO\". It changes the moment the context\n864\t  changes. Each local player's strip sits in their own corner.\n865\t\n866\t### 9.4 Who controls whom\n867\t- **Offense, one human:** the QB through the snap. After a handoff or pitch he controls the carrier. After a throw he\n868\t  controls the **target receiver** (camera and control follow the ball). After a catch, the catcher.\n869\t- **Offense, two humans (co-op):** player A has the QB, then the ball. Player B picks a receiver at the play call\n870\t  (□ cycles the highlighted receiver on his panel; default WR1) and runs it himself; the route line shows as a guide.\n871\t  If B's receiver catches the ball, B has the ball and A takes the nearest teammate (to block). Otherwise B keeps his\n872\t  receiver (to block downfield).\n873\t- **Defense:** each human controls one defender. At PRESNAP the first human gets the S (a safety sees the whole play\n874\t  and can roam) and the second gets LB1, both of which can be moved before the snap. During the play, b2 or a click\n875\t  switches to the free defender nearest the ball (on a pass in the air: the one nearest the landing point). With the **Auto-switch** setting (on by default), a\n876\t  pass in the air or a carrier past the line switches each defensive human to the best unheld defender, with 0.5 s\n877\t  hysteresis.\n878\t- **Turnovers:** at an INT or a fumble recovery, the humans of the side that gained the ball take the carrier (the\n879\t  first human) and the nearest teammates. The other side's humans take their nearest defenders.\n880\t- **Kicks:** the kicking human runs the meter. The receiving human controls the returner.\n881\t- **Hub sessions:** the same rules, keyed by seat (§16.4).\n882\t- A ring under each held athlete in the player's color (local: `E.PLAYER_COLORS`; sessions: the seat color), and a\n883\t  tag over him (\"P1\", or the hub name) during PRESNAP and for 1 s after each switch.\n884\t\n885\t### 9.5 The mouse on the field\n886\t- Every athlete is drawn with `r.actor(..., { pick: athleteId })`. Each step, `r.pick(...input.pointer())` gives the\n887\t  hovered athlete. Hovering a receiver while QB highlights his chip, and a left click (`input.clicked()`) throws to\n888\t  him (Shift held: a Rocket Pass). Hovering a defender on defense outlines him, and a click switches to him.\n889\t- Pre-snap on offense, a click on the QB or the ball hikes.\n890\t- During the kick meter, a click anywhere presses it.\n891\t- Clicking open ground does nothing in play. A click is never bound to b1, which avoids accidental hikes and dives.\n892\t- The pointer hides after 2 s without mouse movement. `input.lastDevice` switches back to the keyboard on the next\n893\t  key.\n894\t\n895\t---\n896\t\n897\t## 10. The over-the-top systems (`36-ots.js`)\n898\t\n899\t### 10.1 Hot Streak (\"BLAZING!\")"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"d5d24edc-58e0-45ff-b4d0-71ca4739d54a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:06:14.868Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-070556__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","content":"**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players\n(about 1.9–2.2 yd tall at rig sizes 1.2–1.45) and the store's props stay in proportion. z is always up. Angles are\nradians, 0 east. `FF.u.yd(n) = n * 16`.\n\n### 5.2 Field geometry\n- The playing field is 100 yards between the goal lines, plus 10-yard end zones, so 120 × 53 yards.\n- The TileMap is 128 columns × 70 rows. Columns 0–3 are outside the left end, 4–123 are the 120 field yards (end\n  zones at 4–13 and 114–123), and 124–127 are outside the right end.\n- Rows from the top: 0–7 are the far stands (8 tiers), 8–11 the far sideline area, 12–64 the 53 field rows, and 65–69\n  the near sideline (benches, cheerleaders, photographers, chain gang).\n- World constants (in `25-field.js`): `END_L = 64` (the left end line, x), `GOAL_L = 224`, `GOAL_R = 1824`,\n  `END_R = 1984`, `SIDE_T = 192` (the far sideline, y), `SIDE_B = 1040` (the near sideline, y), `MID_Y = 616`.\n- Yard lines run every 5 yards. Numbers every 10 sit 12 yards in from each sideline. Hash marks every yard sit at\n  `MID_Y ± 3 yd` (arcade hashes, close to the middle). A team abbreviation is painted in each end zone, and the home\n  logo at midfield.\n- The ball spot after a play: x where the ball was when it died, y clamped to the hashes.\n- A team attacking right has `dir = +1`; attacking left, `dir = -1`. Field position \"own 20\" for a team attacking right\n  is x = `GOAL_L + 20*16`. `FF.sim.yardline(x, side)` converts both ways.\n- Goalposts sit at each end line (x = END_L, END_R; y = MID_Y): a base post, a gooseneck 1 yd toward the field, the\n  crossbar at **53** units (10 ft), and uprights **99** units apart (18.5 ft) rising **160** above the bar. Draw them\n  with `r.queue` lines like the SPORTS kit's posts, 2 px wide, in yellow `#ffd84a` with a `#c89a1a` shaded side. They\n  are drawn, not shapes, because they need exact proportions and a hit test for kicks.\n- Pylons sit at the 4 goal-line and 4 end-line corners (shape `pylon`, §12.6).\n\n### 5.3 The TileMap\n- Legend: `.` grass (floor tag `grass`), `e` end zone (`endzone`), `s` sideline (`sideline`), `m` mud (`mud`), `i` ice\n  (`ice`), `,` outside (`outside`), and `1`–`8` the stand tiers (wall types 11–18).\n- Wall types 11–18: heights 14, 26, 38, 50, 62, 74, 86, 98, each 1 tile deep, with\n  `cut: true, cutH: 8` so reversed and rotated views see past them. Faces and roofs come from the stadium\n  (`face: 'plank'` bleachers at Tumbleweed, `'stone'` concrete at the Mega Dome, `roof: 'slab'`).\n- Tiers rise away from the field. Row 7, the front row next to the far sideline area, is type 11 (h 14), and each row\n  further back is one type higher, up to row 0 = type 18 (h 98). A stadium with N tiers (§18.2: 3 to 8) fills rows\n  8−N to 7. The row behind its top tier (7−N) is the back wall, and any rows above that are `outside`.\n- Behind the top tier is a back wall (type 19, h 120) with banners (props) and, by stadium, a scoreboard or jumbotron\n  shape.\n- Side stands behind each end zone (columns 0–3) are 4 tiers (types 11–14), so an end zone view has a crowd too.\n- `outside`: the stadium's ground color beyond the map. The sky comes from an `E.Backdrop` drawn first in `draw`\n  (mesas, bayou trees, a skyline, mountains with the northern lights, the dome's ceiling truss). It shows above the\n  far stands when the camera looks high.\n\n### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n  runtime (§11.5).\n- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n  between the hashes.\n- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n\n### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\nEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\ncooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\nmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\nbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\nprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n\n### 5.6 Views\n| Id | Made as | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n\nSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\nthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n\n### 6.1 The match state machine\n```\nCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\nDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n```\n- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n  scenario's. The winner receives.\n- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n  special cards.\n- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n\n### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n  on downs at the spot.\n- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n\n### 6.3 Scoring\n| Score | Points | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n\n### 6.4 Kicks\n- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n\n### 6.5 The clock\n- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n  dead.\n\n### 6.6 Turnovers and possession changes\n- **Interception:** the defender has the ball live and can return it. Control flips (§9.4).\n- **Fumble:** the ball goes loose (§7.7). Whoever recovers has possession. A recovery by the defense is live (can be\n  returned). A recovery by the offense is live too (arcade).\n- **Lateral or backward pass:** legal any time. A bad lateral that hits the ground is a loose ball (a fumble).\n- **Turnover on downs:** the other side takes the ball at the spot.\n- After a change of possession, the line of scrimmage, `dir` and the down reset for the new offense.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Athletes, movement and actions\n\n### 7.1 Ratings\nEach athlete has ratings 1–10: `SPD` speed, `ACC` acceleration and agility (cuts, spins), `STR` strength (blocking,\nbreaking tackles), `HND` hands (catching, ball security), `TCK` tackling, `ARM` throw power, `AIM` throw accuracy,\n`AWR` awareness (AI reaction and reads), `KCK` kicking (power and steadiness). Team ratings on the team-select screen\nare 4 bars averaged from the roster: PASS, RUN, DEFENSE, SPECIAL.\n\n### 7.2 Movement\n- Top speed in units/s = `T.speedBase` (92) + SPD × `T.speedPerSpd` (7), so SPD 2 is 106 (about 6.6 yd/s, a lineman)\n  and SPD 10 is 162 (10.1 yd/s, Zap Zapata).\n- Carrying the ball costs −4%. Backpedaling (moving against `facing` by more than 100°) costs −35%.\n- Acceleration = `T.accelBase` (520) + ACC × 30 units/s². Braking is 900. Turn rate is 9 rad/s at speed and 16 rad/s\n  slow (`E.approachAng`).\n- **Turbo** (the `turbo` action, held): × `T.turboMult` (1.25) speed and × 1.3 acceleration. It drains `T.turboDrain`\n  (0.45/s of a full bar) and regenerates `T.turboRegen` (0.22/s) when not held. Each dead ball refills +35%.\n  Big Hits need turbo ≥ 0.15 and cost 0.15. Every athlete has a bar; the HUD shows the bars of human-held athletes.\n- Surfaces: `mud` × 0.85 speed and × 0.7 acceleration, dives slide 1.5× farther. `ice` × 0.7 grip: velocity\n  approaches the desired one at half the rate (slides), and cuts are wider. Snow weather × 0.95 speed. These come from\n  the floor tag under the athlete (`map.floorAt`).\n- Bodies are `E.Body` (r 6; linemen r 7.5; gravity 700) with `body.update(dt, map)` for z, jumps and the stands.\n  The sim sets `vx, vy` from intents each step.\n\n### 7.3 Collisions between athletes\n- Circle against circle every step for every pair (91 pairs at 14 athletes; no hash needed). Separate along the\n  normal in proportion to the inverse of mass (mass = 1 + STR × 0.15; linemen get +0.6).\n- Teammates push gently. Opponents push hard: no overlap.\n- An overlap with the ball carrier during LIVE is a **contact** and may start a tackle (§7.5). An overlap between a\n  blocker and a rusher starts an **engagement** (§7.9).\n- Refs and sideline cast don't collide with the sim. Refs are pushed aside cosmetically and fall comically if a play\n  runs through them (§10.8).\n\n### 7.4 The action system\nEach athlete has at most one action at a time: `{ id, t, dur, phase, data }`. Actions lock movement as their table\nsays, set the athlete's **pose code** (Appendix C) for the presentation, and resolve at their key moment.\n\n| Action | Who | Input | Duration | Key moment and effect |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `hike` | C | the offense's b1 in PRESNAP | 0.25 s | the ball to the QB (shotgun: an arc in 0.3 s; under center: in hand) |\n| `handoff` | QB → RB | automatic on run plays at the play's `handoffT` | 0.35 s | the ball changes hands when within 1.2 yd; a pitch or toss is a short arc |\n| `throw` | QB | a receiver button, or a click on a receiver | 0.28 s wind + release | the ball's flight (§7.6); with turbo held, a Rocket Pass |\n| `dive` | carrier | b1 | 0.45 s air + 0.6 s down | a lunge of 2.5 yd at 1.4× speed; the ball spot is where he lands; the play ends (down) unless he's in the end zone, which is a TD |\n| `spin` | carrier | b3 | 0.32 s | evade window 0.0–0.22 s: a contact from a tackler that isn't behind him (angle > 110° from his facing) misses; speed × 0.8 during the spin; cooldown 0.8 s |\n| `stiffArm` | carrier | b2 | 0.4 s | a tackler in front (within 60° of facing, within 1.8 yd) gets the break chance + `T.stiffBonus` (0.35); on a break the tackler stumbles (0.6 s down) |\n| `hurdle` | carrier | b4 | 0.55 s (`body.jump(260)`) | airborne: low dives (`dive`, `tackleDive`) pass under him; a standing tackler isn't evaded |\n| `lateral` | carrier | lb or rb | 0.25 s | pitch to the nearest teammate behind or beside him (within 100° of the backward direction, ≤ 9 yd), a 0.35–0.6 s arc; a bad lateral (no teammate in range) is thrown to the nearest spot and is a loose ball |\n| `tackleDive` | defender | b1 (the AI decides when, §8.4) | 0.45 s lunge + 0.7 s down on a miss | contact with the carrier during the lunge resolves a tackle (§7.5) |\n| `bigHit` | defender | turbo + b1 | 0.35 s lunge at 1.6× speed | as a tackle with `T.bigHitBonus` (+0.12) and a fumble roll × 3; a miss is 1.0 s down |\n| `strip` | defender | b3 | 0.3 s | within 1.2 yd of the carrier: fumble chance `T.stripChance` (0.12) − HND × 0.01; otherwise a whiff |\n| `jump` | any | b4 | 0.5 s | a jump for a catch, an INT or a swat: +16 units reach height and +6 catch radius for the ball arriving during 0.12–0.42 s |\n| `diveCatch` | receiver | b1 while the ball is in the air | 0.45 s | +10 catch radius for low balls (arrival z < 12); he ends on the ground |\n| `kick` | K or P | the meter (§7.8) | 0.6 s | the ball's flight from the meter's power and accuracy |\n| `lateHit` | anyone | b1 within `T.lateHitWindow` after the whistle, an opponent within 2 yd | 0.5 s | cosmetic knockdown, crowd boos and cheers, the announcer's outrage, the \"Late hits\" stat |\n| `celebrate` | scorer | auto or b2/b3/b4 | 3.5 s | dance (§10.6) |\n| `getUp` | anyone downed | auto | 0.6–1.0 s | back on his feet |\n\nInputs are buffered for 0.15 s (`input.buffered` + `consume`) so a press slightly early still happens.\n\n### 7.5 Tackle resolution\nWhen a tackler's lunge, or an engaged contact, touches the carrier (centers within r1 + r2 + 4):\n```\np = T.tackleBase (0.62) + 0.03*TCK(t) - 0.015*STR(c) - 0.012*ACC(c)\n  + (fromBehind ? 0.15 : 0) + (bigHit ? T.bigHitBonus : 0) + (carrier diving or hurdling ? +0.25 : 0)\n  + bus.mod('tackle.p', ctx)    // cards, Hot Streak, Dinged, specials, difficulty\np = clamp(p, 0.15, 0.97)\n```\n- Evasions are checked first: a spin's window, a stiff arm's frontal break, a hurdle against a low dive.\n- Success: the carrier goes down (pose `TACKLED`, falling the way the tackle pushed him), the ball is dead where it\n  is, and the tackler lands on him. On a **Big Hit**, the carrier is launched along the hit direction at\n  `T.bigHitLaunch` (180 u/s) and up at 150 u/s (pose `FLY`). Hit-stop 0.08 s, shake 4, a comic \"BOOM!\" (§11.3).\n  The fumble roll is `T.fumbleBase` (0.03) × (bigHit 3) × (rain or snow 1.6) × (1 − HND × 0.05) + mods. A fumbled\n  ball comes loose at the moment of the hit (§7.7).\n- Failure: the tackler's lunge carries on and he lands (0.7 s down; 1.0 after a Big Hit). The carrier loses 25% of\n  his speed for 0.3 s (a stumble) and gets a \"BROKEN TACKLE!\" pop when he was going ≥ 60% of top speed.\n- **Gang tackles:** 2+ tacklers in contact within 0.3 s get +0.18 each beyond the first.\n\n### 7.6 The ball (`28-ball.js`)\nStates: `held` (by an athlete), `snap`, `pass`, `kick`, `loose`, `dead`.\n- **Held:** at the holder's carry point (sim: 6 units in front of the hip at z 14). The presentation draws it at the\n  rig's hand through the gear hook (§12.2).\n- **Pass:** analytic flight. At the throw, pick the target point P = the receiver's predicted position at the arrival\n  time (iterate twice: t = d / speed). Speed by type: lob `T.lobSpeed` (240) + ARM × 8 u/s along the ground, bullet\n  (Rocket Pass) `T.bulletSpeed` (400) + ARM × 10. Height at release is 30, and the ball aims to arrive at chest\n  height (z 20): `E.arc({ x, y, z: 30 }, { x: P.x, y: P.y, z: 20 }, T.passGravity (300), { time: t })` gives the\n  velocity. Passes use their own gravity, 300 (loose balls use 600), so the arcs read well. A 30-yd bullet (about\n  1.2 s) peaks about 3 yd up, and a lob (about 1.6 s) about 6 yd up, a rainbow over the defender.\n  - **Accuracy:** P gets a gaussian offset with σ = `T.aimError` (0.035) × distance × (1.3 − AIM × 0.06) × (pressure:\n    a rusher within 2 yd 1.6; throwing on the run 1.25) + mods.\n  - **Max range:** `T.maxPass` (55 yd) + ARM × 1.5 yd. Beyond it the ball is short.\n  - **In flight:** the ball spins (draw the spiral, §12.7). A marker shows the landing point on the ground for the\n    receiving team's human and the defending human (an X in team color, with a ring that shrinks as it nears).\n  - **Arrival** (the ball at z ≤ 34 near P): resolve the catch (§7.7). If no one is in range, or the ball hits the\n    ground, the pass is incomplete (a bounce: a short random-direction `loose` bounce for 0.6 s, cosmetic, dead).\n- **Kick:** analytic flight to the landing point the meter gives, by `E.arc(from, landing, T.kickGravity (260),\n  { apex })`, with apex `T.kickApex` (punts 260, kickoffs 220, FGs and PATs 150, squibs 20, onside 60). The hang time\n  follows: a punt is about 2.8 s. A high kick may leave the top of the screen. The KICK shot zooms out to 0.75, and\n  the shadow and landing marker keep the ball readable. On landing it goes `loose`: the `E.Body` ball, bounce 0.45,\n  with friction. A football bounces erratically, so on each bounce add a random ±25° turn and ±30% speed from\n  `FF.rng.sim`.\n- **Loose:** an `E.Body` (r 3, bounce 0.45, gravity 600) with erratic bounces as above. Players dive at it (§7.7).\n- Out of bounds (y outside the sidelines, or past an end line) kills the ball at the crossing point. A kick out of\n  bounds follows §6.4.\n\n### 7.7 Catches, interceptions, deflections, fumbles and pile-ups\n**Catch resolution** at arrival. Candidates are athletes whose reach covers the ball. The horizontal distance must be\n≤ `T.catchRadius` (14) (+6 jumping in window, +10 diving), and the arrival height must be ≤ reach (34 standing, 50\njumping). For each candidate:\n```\noffense: pc = T.catchBase (0.66) + 0.03*HND - 0.22*(defenders within 14) - (bullet ? 0.10 : 0)\n             + (jump in window ? 0.08 : 0) + bus.mod('catch.p')        // Glue Gloves, Butterfingers, Hot Streak\ndefense: pi = 0.10 + 0.035*HND + (jump in window ? 0.12 : 0) + (in front of receiver ? 0.10 : 0) + bus.mod('int.p')\n```\n- Order the candidates by distance to the ball. Each nearest defender rolls first: INT with `pi`, else a\n  **deflection** with 0.35 (the ball pops up to z 20–40 with a random velocity and can be caught again).\n- Then the receiver rolls `pc`: a catch, or a **drop** (\"DROPPED!\", the ball bounces dead).\n- Cheats and specials can force outcomes: `forceCatch`, `forceINT`, Golden Spiral (no INT).\n- A catch gives the receiver the ball live with his velocity kept (catch and run). Control follows (§9.4).\n\n**Fumbles and loose balls.** The ball pops out with a velocity away from the hit plus up 60–110 u/s. Every athlete\nwithin `T.pileRadius` (5 yd) of the ball gets the brain behavior `recover` (dive at the ball). Humans dive with b1.\n- The first athlete whose dive or feet touch the ball while z ≤ 6 gets a **recovery**. Rolled, not instant: if 2+\n  athletes are within 1.5 yd at that moment, a **pile-up** starts.\n- **Pile-up** (1.6 s): every athlete within 2.5 yd of the ball dives in (poses `PILE`, stacked by z offsets), a dust\n  or mud cloud, the camera punches in, the crowd \"OOOH\". Then the ref \"digs\" (comic) and signals possession, a roll\n  weighted by each piler's distance to the ball when it started (closer is more likely) and STR. \"WHO'S GOT IT?! ...\n  THE FIASCOS HAVE IT!\"\n- A loose ball that goes out of bounds belongs to the team that last had it.\n\n### 7.8 Kicking and the kick meter\nA 3-press meter in screen space near the kicker (§14.7):\n1. b1 starts the needle sweeping a power bar. It rises at `T.meterSpeed` (1.4 sweeps/s), then falls back.\n2. b1 again sets the **power** (0–1). Past 0.95 is the red zone: +5% distance but the accuracy needle runs 1.5×\n   faster.\n3. The accuracy needle sweeps across a center band. b1 sets **accuracy** (−1..1). |acc| ≤ the band\n   (`T.accBand` 0.12; Super Foot ×2.5) is straight; otherwise the kick hooks by acc × 9° (FG) or acc × 14° (punt\n   and kickoff).\n- Before step 1 the stick aims: ±15° for punts and kickoffs, ±4° for FGs (the Kick Cam shows the aim line).\n- Distance: FG = 25 + power × (25 + KCK × 1.6) yd, so KCK 7 at full power is about 61 yd, before wind. Punt: 30–55\n  yd. Kickoff: as §6.4.\n- The FG test: when the ball reaches the end line's x, it's good if y is between the uprights (±49.5) and z > 53.\n  A ball off an upright (within 3 units) bounces \"DOINK!\" and is no good (or good if it falls inside: roll it).\n- CPU kickers: power and accuracy from KCK and difficulty, plus gaussian noise (σ 0.08 / 0.10).\n- A mouse left click, b1 and Space all press the meter.\n\n### 7.9 Blocking\n- **Engagement:** a blocker and a rusher overlap, or come within 1.5 yd with the blocker facing him, which locks them\n  as a pair: they hold 0.8 yd apart, facing each other.\n- **Contest:** each step the pair moves together with velocity `T.blockPush` (18 u/s) × (STR_r − STR_b +\n  rushMoveBonus + (turbo ? 2 : 0)) toward the rusher's goal (the QB or the carrier) when positive, or the blocker's\n  drive direction when negative. Run blocks add the blocker's drive (+1.5).\n- **Shed:** the rusher sheds when his shed timer runs out. `shedTime = T.shedBase (1.9 s) × (STR_b / STR_r) ×\n  rand(0.7, 1.3)`, or ∞ under Brick Wall. On a shed the rusher gets a 0.3 s burst (×1.2) toward his target, and the\n  blocker staggers 0.3 s. A human rusher can mash b3 (□) to cut his timer by 0.12 s per press (a swim move; max 6).\n- **Pancake:** when STR_b − STR_r ≥ 3, a 15% roll on engagement knocks the rusher down for 1.2 s. \"PANCAKE!\"\n- Pass blocks mirror the most dangerous unblocked rusher in the blocker's zone (left, middle, right). Run blocks\n  follow the play's assignment (§8.2).\n- A human-controlled lineman (co-op, or by switching) moves freely. Engagement then happens on contact.\n\n---\n\n## 8. The AI (`33-ai.js`, `34-ai-coach.js`)\n\n### 8.1 Brains and behaviors\nEvery athlete not held by a human (or by a remote player) has a brain. A brain picks a **behavior** from its play\nassignment and the phase, re-thinks at 20 Hz (staggered: `(step + index) % 6 === 0`), and every step turns the current\nbehavior into an intent (a move vector, turbo, presses). Reaction delays come from `AWR` and difficulty: an AI sees a\n**delayed** world. It reads other athletes' positions from a 0.1–0.4 s history buffer, which makes cutbacks, jukes\nand play fakes work against it.\n\n| Behavior | Role | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `formation` | all, PRESNAP | jog to the formation spot, face the line, take the stance |\n| `passBlock(zone)` | OL, RB on some plays | hold a spot 1.5–2.5 yd in front of the QB in his zone, mirror the most dangerous rusher there, engage |\n| `runBlock(target, drive)` | OL, WR, TE | engage the assigned defender and drive him along `drive`, or climb to the 2nd level |\n| `pull(path, then)` | G on sweeps and counters | run the path, then lead-block the first defender in front of the carrier |\n| `route(points, after)` | WR, RB | run the waypoints (cut sharply: brake to 60% at a break, 0.12 s), then `after`: `settle` (find the nearest hole in the zones, face the QB), `scramble` (work toward the sideline the QB scrambles to) or `block` |\n| `carry(path)` | the carrier | follow the play's path while it's open, then **free running**: score 16 directions each 0.1 s, `forward·w1 − Σ threat(defender) − sideline penalty − crowding`, where threat = closing speed / distance² for defenders within 8 yd; pick the best with hysteresis; turbo when no defender is within 4 yd or one is closing from behind; evasive moves, below |\n| `qbPass(drop, reads)` | QB | drop back to the play's spot. From `readT` (0.6–1.0 s) read the receivers in order. **Openness** = the distance from the nearest defender to the receiver's arrival point, minus 8 per defender within 3 yd of the pass lane. Throw to the first receiver with openness > `T.aiOpen` (3 yd; by difficulty) whose route is ≥ 40% done. At 2.8 s, or with a rusher within 2 yd, throw to the best, scramble if the best is < 1 yd open, or throw it away out of bounds (Pro and Fiasco difficulty). Scramble: `carry`, and still look to throw while behind the line. |\n| `qbRun` | QB on sneaks and the run fakes of draws | his part of the play script |\n| `rush(gap)` | DL, blitzers | attack the gap toward the QB, engage blockers, try to shed; once free, `pursue` the QB; when the QB throws, `pursue` the ball |\n| `manCover(target)` | DB, LB | keep a cushion of `T.cushion` (2 yd, smaller near the goal line) on the goal side of the target, matching his speed with the reaction delay; when the ball is in the air, break to the arrival point after the reaction delay and jump when within 2 yd as it arrives |\n| `zoneCover(zone)` | DB, LB | drop to the zone center; pick the most threatening receiver inside (zone radius + 3 yd), weighted by depth and how open he is; shadow him; release him when he leaves; the ball in the air is as above |\n| `spy(QB)` | LB | mirror the QB at 5 yd; when the QB crosses the line, `pursue` |\n| `pursue(carrier)` | all defenders, once there is a carrier past the line, a scramble, or a return | head for the intercept point: the smallest t with `|c + v_c·t − d| ≤ speed_d·t`, solved in 0.05 s steps up to 3 s, with the angle limited by the sideline. Tackle decision below |\n| `kickCover(lane)` | the kicking team | sprint downfield in lanes 6 yd apart, then `pursue` the returner |\n| `returnBlock` | the receiving team | form a wedge of 3 in front of the returner's lane; engage the nearest coverage man |\n| `recover` | everyone near a loose ball | dive at the ball (§7.7) |\n| `celebrate`, `dejected`, `idle` | after the play | poses (§10.6) |\n\n**Evasive moves for an AI carrier.** When a tackler is within 1.6 yd and closing: against a diving tackler, hurdle\nwith chance 0.25 + ACC × 0.03; against a frontal one, stiff arm with 0.2 + STR × 0.03; against one from the side,\nspin with 0.2 + ACC × 0.03. One move per 0.8 s. Difficulty scales these.\n\n**The tackle decision (AI tacklers).** With a reachable carrier, dive when the distance is under 1.8 yd and the angle\nto his predicted position is under 35°. Use a Big Hit when turbo ≥ 0.15 and a roll of 0.2 + TCK × 0.03 passes (×1.5\nwhen Hot). Otherwise keep pursuing to contact (a wrap-up on contact counts as a tackle attempt with −0.08).\n\n### 8.2 Play assignments (data to brain)\n`FF.data.plays[id].assign[slot]` (Appendix A.2) is read when PRESNAP ends:\n- Offense: `{ block: 'pass' | 'run', zone, target, drive }`, `{ pull: [[x, y], ...], then: 'lead' }`,\n  `{ route: [[x, y], ...], after: 'settle', read: n }`, `{ carry: [[x, y], ...], handoffT }`, and the QB's\n  `{ drop: [x, y], reads: ['WR2', 'WR1', 'RB'], readT, fake }`.\n- Defense: `{ rush: 'A' | 'B' | 'C' | 'contain' }`, `{ man: 'WR1' | 'WR2' | 'RB' | 'C' }`,\n  `{ zone: { at: [x, y], r } }`, `{ spy: 'QB' }`.\n- Play coordinates are in **yards** from the ball spot: x downfield (+ is the offense's attacking direction), y\n  across (+ is the offense's right). Mirroring (FLIP) negates y. Defense coordinates use the same frame (x is the\n  distance past the line toward the defense's own goal). The sim converts with `dir`.\n- Targets in defense assignments point at offensive slots. If the offense's formation lacks a slot, fall back to\n  zone `[8, 0], r 7`.\n\n### 8.3 Special teams brains\nKick coverage, wedge returns, the punt shield (3 blockers in front of the punter for 1.5 s), FG protection\n(pass-block in place) and the FG rush (all-out `rush`; 2 jumpers in the middle `jump` at the kick).\n\n### 8.4 Difficulty\n| | Rookie | Pro | Fiasco |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CPU reaction delay | 0.38 s | 0.24 s | 0.13 s |\n| CPU tackle p × | 0.85 | 1.0 | 1.12 |\n| CPU QB openness threshold | 4.5 yd | 3 yd | 2 yd |\n| CPU kick noise × | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.6 |\n| CPU coverage cushion × | 1.3 | 1.0 | 0.85 |\n| Catch-up assist (`T.rubberBand`): when the human trails by ≥ 14, his team gets +0.05 catch, +0.04 tackle and −0.04 to the CPU's | on | off | off |\nStory chapters set a difficulty per chapter (§15.1), scaled by the Difficulty setting.\n\n### 8.5 The coach AI (play calling, cards, decisions)\n- **Situation features:** down, distance, field position, score difference, time left, quarter, and the opponent's\n  last 5 play types (Fiasco difficulty only).\n- **Offense:** start from the team's tendency weights (`ai.run`, `ai.pass`, `ai.trick`, §18.1), then apply the\n  situation rules in order:\n  - 3rd/4th & 10+ means pass deep or mid (×2.5).\n  - Less than 3 to go means run, sneak or quick passes (×2).\n  - Goal to go inside the 5 means GOAL LINE runs, slants and fades.\n  - In the last 1:00 while trailing: sideline routes (Quick Outs, Out & Up) ×3, and get out of bounds.\n  - Leading by 9+ in the last 2:00: runs and kneels.\n  - 4th down: field goal if the kick is ≤ `T.cpuFgRange` (45 yd) + KCK. Go for it if the distance is ≤ 3 and the ball\n    is past midfield, or if trailing late. Punt otherwise. A 5% fake punt roll on 4th & ≤ 5.\n- **Defense:** counter the offense's tendency (blitz on 3rd & long ×1.8, goal line inside the 3, prevent while\n  leading late), with 20% randomness so the CPU can be read only roughly.\n- **Cards:** the CPU uses a card when its trigger matches (§10.2) and it holds one. At most one card per 3 plays.\n- **PAT:** go for 2 when down by 2, 5, 9, 10 or 12 after the TD in the second half. Otherwise kick.\n- **COACH SAYS:** run the offense logic for the human's side, and mark the top pick with a star badge on the\n  play-call screen. If the human doesn't choose, it plays when the timer runs out.\n\n### 8.6 The Play Lab (an AI quality tool and test)\n`FF.dev.playLab(offPlay, defPlay, n, { seed, teams, difficulty })` runs n plays headless and fast (presentation off)\nfrom midfield on 1st & 20, both sides CPU. It returns `{ avgYards, completion, sacks, ints, fumbles, tds, avgTime,\nmaxTime, stuck }`, where `stuck` counts plays over 25 s or an athlete idle more than 3 s while LIVE.\n**The bands it must stay in** (every offensive play against each of the 8 non-kick defenses, n = 30, Pro, Fiascos vs\nMudhogs):\n- Nothing is `stuck`, and every play ends within 25 s.\n- A pass play's completion, averaged over the 8 defenses, is 35–85% (HAIL MARY 5–40%), and its sack rate is under\n  25%.\n- A run play averages 1.5–9 yd over the 8 defenses.\n- No play averages over 25 yd over the 8 defenses (no unstoppable play), and no defense holds every play under 1 yd\n  (no unbeatable defense).\nIn the browser the Dev menu runs the same lab on screen with debug overlays (§17).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Controls and human control (`35-control.js`)\n\n### 9.1 The input map (`FF.KEYS`)\nA custom preset (scene `input: FF.KEYS`). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives a shared-keyboard half its 6\nbuttons in this order. The engine's standard `confirm`/`cancel` are redefined so J and K don't mean menu\nconfirm/cancel.\n```js\nFF.KEYS = {\n  up: E.Input.DEFAULT.up, down: E.Input.DEFAULT.down, left: E.Input.DEFAULT.left, right: E.Input.DEFAULT.right,\n  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter', 'Space', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1'],\n  b1: ['Space', 'KeyK', 'Pad0'],   // ✕ / A    hike, dive, tackle, kick meter, pick\n  b2: ['KeyL', 'Pad1'],            // ○ / B    stiff arm, switch player, back\n  b3: ['KeyJ', 'Pad2'],            // □ / X    spin, strip, flip play\n  b4: ['KeyI', 'Pad3'],            // △ / Y    hurdle, jump, catch, Fiasco Card\n  turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7'],   // R2 / RT\n  lb: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],            // L1 / LB  lateral, previous tab\n  rb: ['KeyE', 'Pad5'],            // R1 / RB  lateral, next tab\n  r1: ['Digit1'], r2: ['Digit2'], r3: ['Digit3'], r4: ['Digit4']   // keyboard: throw to receiver 1-4\n};\n```\nCheck `E.Input.DEFAULT` for the exact move codes (WASD, the arrows, the d-pad, `PadAxis*`). The Controls screen names\nthem with `menu.controls: { b1: 'Hike · Dive · Tackle · Kick', b2: 'Stiff arm · Switch player', b3: 'Spin · Strip ·\nFlip play', b4: 'Hurdle · Jump · Catch · Card', turbo: 'Turbo', lb: 'Lateral · Tab left', rb: 'Lateral · Tab right',\nr1: 'Throw to receiver 1', ... }`.\n\n### 9.2 The control table by context\n| Context | Action | PlayStation | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Menus | move the focus | d-pad / left stick | d-pad / left stick | arrows / WASD | hover moves the focus |\n| | select | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | left click |\n| | back | ○ | B | Esc / Backspace | the on-screen BACK button |\n| | tabs | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | click a tab |\n| Play call | pick the play | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | click a card |\n| | flip it | □ | X | J | click FLIP |\n| | Fiasco Card | △ (opens the card row) | Y | I | click a card slot |\n| Pre-snap, offense | hike | ✕ | A | Space | click the QB |\n| Pre-snap, defense | move your defender | stick | stick | WASD | — |\n| | switch defender | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n| QB behind the line | throw to receiver ✕ ○ □ △ | ✕ ○ □ △ | A B X Y | 1 2 3 4 (or K L J I) | click a receiver |\n| | Rocket Pass | hold R2 + a receiver | RT + | Shift + | Shift + click |\n| | scramble | stick (+ R2) | stick (+ RT) | WASD (+ Shift) | — |\n| Ball carrier | turbo | R2 | RT | Shift | — |\n| | dive | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| | stiff arm | ○ | B | L | — |\n| | spin | □ | X | J | — |\n| | hurdle | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | lateral | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | — |\n| Ball in the air, offense | jump catch | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | diving catch | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| Defense, live | switch to the defender nearest the ball | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n| | tackle (dive) | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| | Big Hit | R2 + ✕ | RT + A | Shift + Space | — |\n| | jump, swat, intercept | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | strip | □ | X | J | — |\n| | swim move (while blocked) | □ repeatedly | X | J | — |\n| After the whistle | late hit | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| Kicking | aim | stick ← → | stick | A / D, ← / → | — |\n| | the meter (3 presses) | ✕ | A | Space | left click |\n| Kick return in the end zone | take a knee | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| TD celebration | pick a dance | ○ □ △ | B X Y | L J I | — |\n| Holding a boss star | his special (§10.5) | L1 + R1 together | LB + RB | Q + E | — |\n| Anywhere | pause | Options | Menu | Esc / P | the corner menu button |\n| Anywhere | change the camera view | — | — | V | — |\nThe mouse wheel zooms the camera within the scene's range (engine standard, `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4] }`).\n\n### 9.3 Prompts that follow the device\n- `FF.glyph.chip(g, x, y, action, { player, scale, device })` draws a button chip for that player's last device\n  (`input.lastDevice`):\n  - **Gamepad, PlayStation** (the default): a dark disc with the symbol drawn as pixels in its classic color: ✕ blue\n    `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ae0a0`.\n  - **Gamepad, Xbox** (Settings, Controller, Button symbols): colored discs with letters: A green, B red, X blue,\n    Y yellow.\n  - Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles labeled L1, R1, R2 or LB, RB, RT. Options/Menu are small pills.\n  - **Keyboard:** a keycap (a light rectangle with a darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`. Wide\n    caps for SPACE and SHIFT.\n  - **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, used where the mouse does that context's job (clicking\n    a receiver, a card, the kick meter). Elsewhere the keyboard chip.\n  - Labels come from `input.label(action, device)` after rebinding. Never hard-code a key. Map the engine's label to\n    a chip style: glyph labels (`✕ ○ □ △ A B X Y L1 ...`) are pad chips, everything else is keycaps.\n- The receiver chips over receivers' heads show b1–b4 on a pad, r1–r4 (1–4) on a keyboard (b1–b4 on a shared-keyboard\n  half), and a mouse cursor badge on the hovered receiver.\n- **The prompt strip** (bottom right, 1–5 chips with words, §14.6) shows the current context's actions only: \"✕ HIKE\",\n  then \"✕ ○ □ THROW · R2 ROCKET\", then \"□ SPIN ○ STIFF △ HURDLE ✕ DIVE R2 TURBO\". It changes the moment the context\n  changes. Each local player's strip sits in their own corner.\n\n### 9.4 Who controls whom\n- **Offense, one human:** the QB through the snap. After a handoff or pitch he controls the carrier. After a throw he\n  controls the **target receiver** (camera and control follow the ball). After a catch, the catcher.\n- **Offense, two humans (co-op):** player A has the QB, then the ball. Player B picks a receiver at the play call\n  (□ cycles the highlighted receiver on his panel; default WR1) and runs it himself; the route line shows as a guide.\n  If B's receiver catches the ball, B has the ball and A takes the nearest teammate (to block). Otherwise B keeps his\n  receiver (to block downfield).\n- **Defense:** each human controls one defender. At PRESNAP the first human gets the S (a safety sees the whole play\n  and can roam) and the second gets LB1, both of which can be moved before the snap. During the play, b2 or a click\n  switches to the free defender nearest the ball (on a pass in the air: the one nearest the landing point). With the **Auto-switch** setting (on by default), a\n  pass in the air or a carrier past the line switches each defensive human to the best unheld defender, with 0.5 s\n  hysteresis.\n- **Turnovers:** at an INT or a fumble recovery, the humans of the side that gained the ball take the carrier (the\n  first human) and the nearest teammates. The other side's humans take their nearest defenders.\n- **Kicks:** the kicking human runs the meter. The receiving human controls the returner.\n- **Hub sessions:** the same rules, keyed by seat (§16.4).\n- A ring under each held athlete in the player's color (local: `E.PLAYER_COLORS`; sessions: the seat color), and a\n  tag over him (\"P1\", or the hub name) during PRESNAP and for 1 s after each switch.\n\n### 9.5 The mouse on the field\n- Every athlete is drawn with `r.actor(..., { pick: athleteId })`. Each step, `r.pick(...input.pointer())` gives the\n  hovered athlete. Hovering a receiver while QB highlights his chip, and a left click (`input.clicked()`) throws to\n  him (Shift held: a Rocket Pass). Hovering a defender on defense outlines him, and a click switches to him.\n- Pre-snap on offense, a click on the QB or the ball hikes.\n- During the kick meter, a click anywhere presses it.\n- Clicking open ground does nothing in play. A click is never bound to b1, which avoids accidental hikes and dives.\n- The pointer hides after 2 s without mouse movement. `input.lastDevice` switches back to the keyboard on the next\n  key.\n\n---\n\n## 10. The over-the-top systems (`36-ots.js`)\n\n### 10.1 Hot Streak (\"BLAZING!\")","numLines":540,"startLine":360,"totalLines":2515}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HHwrMujL3AvggbtdG1a85o","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
