{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi7tu1VMHUvQRbU78Kmc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO**. It is in `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-070556.html` (1.9 MB, runs from `file://`), with `exit-interview.json` next to it; that file parses and every field is answered. The full test command passes (940 checks in about a minute) and the AI Hub harness passes all four of its configurations on the final file.\n\n**What's in it:** 7-on-7 arcade football with the Tumbleweed Junction Fiascos and five other teams, across five stadiums with rain, snow, mud, night lights and Lights Out.\n- **The play:** you pick plays from big cards (with a flip and a \"Coach Says\" pick), hike, then run with turbo, spin, stiff arm, hurdle, dive and lateral. You throw by pressing a receiver's button or clicking him; turbo makes it a Rocket Pass.\n- **Hits and turnovers:** Big Hits and Mega Hits send helmets flying, plus fumbles, pile-ups, late hits and a three-press kick meter with a Kick Cam.\n- **The over-the-top layer:** Hot Streaks, the Fiasco Meter with 8 Fiasco Cards, six boss specials, mascots, refs who get knocked over, replays and a highlight reel.\n- **Modes:** a story with a prologue and 5 chapters (cutscenes with gibberish voices, halftime choices, loss and retry, credits), 9 practice drills, exhibition for 1–4 local players, Hall of Fiasco records and an About screen.\n- **Required features:** the attract mode starts after 10 s idle, Dev mode is reachable from the pause menu (live sliders with reset, cheats, situation jumps, a Play Lab), and touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" gate.\n\n**What I cut or simplified from the plan, so you know:**\n- **Online play:** the host runs the game and the other players see its updates without local prediction (the plan's own fallback). A remote player's kicks are kicked automatically.\n- **Smaller trims:** no marching band, blimp or chain gang, and the office cutscene set was dropped.\n- **Speed:** frame rate is 45–60 at 720p but 22–30 at 1080p on this loaded machine. TASK.md puts frame-rate targets out of scope.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:05:56\n- **Source:** American football (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (7-on-7 American football) with beat-'em-up hit feel and a story campaign. The camera is a sideline broadcast view (a custom 50° angle), with Sky Cam and Blimp options, a Kick Cam behind the kicker for field goals, and field-level, isometric and reverse replay angles.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (modified, see Engine Feedback), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, the my-3D2dge mocap runtime with an 18-clip FIASCO set, Canvas 2D and Web Audio. Tests use Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** my-3D2dge's native style: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at PS1 resolution, code-painted fields, stands and crowds, and five lit stadiums with weather and particles. New football gear (helmets, pads, numbers), logos, play cards and Fiasco Card art.\n- **Animation:** my-3D2dge procedural rigs and E.MOVES, joint post-processing (tilt, roll, lift) for dives, flights and pile-ups, and 18 motion-capture clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU).\n- **Audio:** all in code: 11 original songs with layers and stings, 50 synthesized sound effects, a crowd bed with reactions and chants, and a formant babble synth for Animalese and Simlish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players (host-authoritative, snapshots at 14 Hz); local 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - *Allowed:* headless tests with `game.advance` and `game.debug`, a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command that prints only failures and a count, and screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n  - *Used:* `npm test` (14 suites, 940 checks), which includes the story playthrough from the prologue to the credits, the attract loop, and 2–4 player netcode on a fake hub with latency and loss. Also a Play Lab script for AI balance, Playwright screenshots, the engine's check tool, and the AI Hub harness in all 4 configurations.\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine's and the AI Hub's own documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown. No token or cost figures are visible to me. The session ran about 1 h 25 min (07:06 to about 08:30).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n   - **Headless suites (14):** a new match reused the previous match's play calls; a kick play with no kicker on the field crashed a drill; the touch-only gate unlocked itself; the Big Heads cheat was reverted by its own lock; deep links ran before boot finished; storage was missing in Node; three commentary lines ran over 48 characters; three attract segments didn't always show their key moment; and the Seismic Sack's slam was lost when the play ended first.\n   - **Browser runs and screenshots (about 16):** the field drew black, three crashes, 12 fps, glyphs drawn as \"?\", an overflowing menu, and smaller layout and audio problems.\n   - **Play Lab (balance):** runs averaged 13 yards, passes were sacked in 0.8 s, and there were too many interceptions.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 8:\n   - the black field;\n   - three crashes;\n   - the stale play calls, which broke REMATCH, story retries and every scripted situation;\n   - the kick-play crash;\n   - 12 fps rendering;\n   - a pass rush that sacked nearly every pass.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the rules suite's back-to-back situation runs, which exposed the stale play calls. The scripted story playthrough was the best broad safety net.\n4. **Least helpful test:** my first spin-evasion test, which could never fail as written; I rewrote it. The check tool cycling through stock camera views also told me little, since the game uses its own broadcast view.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the HD Humanoid with the motion-capture runtime, and the exact fixed-step headless `game.advance`. Together they let a 940-check suite and a whole-game playthrough run in about a minute without a browser. The free pause, settings, controls and save screens also saved a lot of work.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - I assumed `E.hex` returns an integer; it returns `[r, g, b]`, which made the field black.\n   - I used `input._used` as my own flag, but the engine uses it internally, which broke input.\n   - I didn't know each camera zoom value makes a new floor bake, so a smoothly zooming camera rebaked the whole field every frame.\n   - I called `game.go` from a deep link before the browser boot finished.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the football simulation (rules, clock, kicks, possession, collisions, blocking and sheds), the AI for all 14 athletes and the CPU coach, then the shared-world netcode.\n4. **The hardest part:** performance and caching behaviour that the API doesn't show, mainly floor bakes per view and zoom with a cache of only 6. Also the size of the API surface: the right helper usually existed, but finding it took grepping.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n\n   | # | Change | Why |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | E1 | Added PlayStation symbols and a few UI characters to both pixel fonts | They were drawn as \"?\" |\n   | E2 | PlayStation symbols by default, Xbox as the option, shown on the Settings screen | TASK.md asks for PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option |\n   | E3 | A gear hook in the HD Humanoid's draw | Pads, jersey numbers and the carried ball, depth-sorted inside the rig |\n   | E4 | A football helmet hat style | A helmet that reads as football from every view |\n   | E5 | A per-letter voice function for `E.Dialog` | The Animalese voices in cutscenes |\n   | E6 | Named audio buses | Separate volumes for voices and the crowd |\n   | E7 | Dev settings kept off the Settings screen; settings rows on any Shell screen | Dev mode's live tuning sliders |\n   | E8 | Open Settings without pausing | AI Hub sessions must not freeze the world |\n   | E10 | Floor-bake cache of 16 instead of 6 | The field was rebaking every frame |\n\n   All are logged in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and applied by `tools/patch-engine.mjs`.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make floor bakes independent of zoom, or document loudly that every zoom level is a full rebake. Also expose the pixel font's glyph bitmaps publicly.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** concrete deliverables with clear checks (the file name, the exit interview's exact shape, the hub harness), and 10-foot UX rules specific enough to act on.\n2. **What was confusing:** TASK.md says to pick the title with an RNG, but STAGE.md says to use the plan's title. Also, the plan's scope is far more than one session at medium effort, so weighing \"follow the plan\", \"TASK.md wins\" and \"no stubs\" took judgment about what to cut.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** believable, balanced 7-on-7 AI, plus shared-world multiplayer that passes the harness. The machine's full `/tmp` crashed headless Chromium until I moved TMPDIR into my own folder.\n4. **What I'd change:** state the expected scale or budget, have the plan mark must/should/could features, say which file decides the title, and mention shared-machine limits such as a full `/tmp`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1224,"cache_read_input_tokens":771490,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1224},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"b14feed6-ac02-4b1c-849a-3a68a895dfb8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:25:09.808Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi7ttdRNVb4FJzRBR9hb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The bundle, exit-interview.json and the four report sections exist, but TASK.md's requirements are not all met. TASK.md asks for a title from 50 brainstormed riffs picked with a real RNG (the agent used the plan's title), and the final reply's Testing Feedback claims were not all verified. More importantly, the transcript shows known gaps against TASK.md and the plan that were presented as cut or fine: the Hall of Fiasco and records are present, but the Sky/Blimp and some stadium props and chain gang were dropped; the multiplayer lacks the client prediction the plan required; the Frostbite-style 1080p performance was reported at 22-30 fps; and the Animalese/cutscene and Play Lab balance targets were not confirmed against the plan's bands. Also, the exit-interview 'usage' field and several feedback answers were written as 'unknown' or estimated without evidence. The agent's own closing summary acknowledges these cuts, so the requirement that every feature in TASK.md be implemented with no stubs is not clearly satisfied."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"774c635b-5a98-4f9f-926f-bd9c83878737","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:25:36.868Z","isSynthetic":true}
