{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi9PMmx7HGCjAXSBYQCc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've addressed the stop hook's points, and the game is now **HAMERICAN FOOTBALL**. Tests pass (942 checks), the AI Hub harness passes all four configurations on the final file, and `exit-interview.json` parses. The deliverable is `HamericanFootball-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-070556.html`, with the dev folder renamed to match.\n\n**What changed since my last reply:**\n- **Title:** TASK.md asks for a title drawn by a real RNG, and TASK.md wins over STAGE.md. I brainstormed 50 riffs on \"American Football\" and drew index 8 with Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`. That renamed the logo, the page title, the AI Hub name and every file name. The Fiascos team, the Fiasco Meter and the Fiasco Cup stay as content.\n- **Online play now has client-side prediction:**\n  - **Prediction:** each client moves its own player at once from its own stick, eases toward the host's position, and snaps back past 2 yards or when the player is down.\n  - **Kicking:** an online player now kicks with the same three-press meter on their own copy; before, their kicks were automatic.\n  - **Test:** a new netcode test checks that the client actually predicts its own player.\n- **Chain gang:** a down marker and chain poles on the near sideline follow the line of scrimmage and the line to gain.\n- **AI balance against the plan's own bands:** checked with a new script (`test/bands.mjs`: every offensive play against 8 defenses, 30 plays each).\n  - **Before:** nine of 17 plays were outside.\n  - **The main causes:** blocks that never broke off once the runner was gone, defenders diving at where the runner *was*, and receivers blocking as well as linemen.\n  - **Now:** all 17 are inside on the last full measurement, plus a targeted 30-play recheck of the two pitch plays (I changed only their data after the full run).\n- **Two points I didn't act on:** the Sky Cam and Blimp views were already in Settings, and TASK.md puts frame-rate targets out of scope. The Usage field stays \"unknown\", as TASK.md instructs for anything I can't confirm.\n\nThe four report sections are below; they match the in-game About screen and `exit-interview.json`.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:05:56\n- **Source:** American football (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (title redrawn with a real RNG per TASK.md: Hamerican Football)\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade sports (7-on-7 American football) with beat-'em-up hit feel and a story campaign. The camera is a sideline broadcast view (custom 50°), with Sky Cam and Blimp options, a Kick Cam for field goals, and field-level, isometric and reverse replay angles.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, the mocap runtime with an 18-clip set, Canvas 2D, Web Audio. Tests use Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native style: HD Humanoid pixel-art rigs at PS1 resolution, code-painted fields, stands and crowds, five lit stadiums with weather. New helmets, pads, numbers, logos, play cards and Fiasco Card art.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and E.MOVES, joint post-processing (tilt, roll, lift), and 18 motion-capture clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU).\n- **Audio:** all in code: 11 original songs with layers and stings, 50 sound effects, a crowd bed with chants, and an Animalese/Simlish babble voice synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players. The host runs the world and sends snapshots at 14 Hz; each client predicts its own player and runs its own play-call screen and kick meter. Also local 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - *Allowed:* headless tests with `game.advance` and `game.debug`, a scripted playthrough, one command that prints only failures and a count, and screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n  - *Used:* `npm test` (14 suites, 942 checks, including the story playthrough from the prologue to the credits, the attract loop, and 2–4 player netcode with latency and loss). Also `test/bands.mjs` (the plan's balance bands at n = 30, all 17 plays inside), Playwright screenshots, the engine's check tool, and the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 pass).\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine and AI Hub documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown. No token or cost figures are visible to me. The session ran about 1 h 40 min (07:06 to about 08:45).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n   - **Headless suites (14):** a new match reusing the previous match's play calls; a crash on a kick play with no kicker; the touch-only gate unlocking itself; the Big Heads cheat being reverted by its own lock; deep links running before boot; storage missing headless; three commentary lines over 48 characters; three attract segments missing their key moment; and the Seismic Sack lost when the play ended first.\n   - **Browser runs (about 16):** a black field, three crashes, 12 fps from field rebakes, \"?\" glyphs, an overflowing menu, and other layout and audio problems.\n   - **Play Lab and the plan's bands (balance):** runs averaging 13 yards, passes sacked in 0.8 s, too many interceptions, and blocks that never broke off, which made the pitch plays average 11–14 yards.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 8: the black field, three crashes, the stale play calls (they broke REMATCH and retries), the kick-play crash, 12 fps, and a pass rush that sacked nearly every pass.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the back-to-back scenario runs in the rules suite, which exposed the stale play calls. The scripted story playthrough was the best broad net.\n4. **Least helpful test:** my first spin test, which could never fail until I rewrote it. The check tool's stock camera views also told me little.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the HD Humanoid with mocap, and exact headless `game.advance`. A whole-game playthrough and 942 checks run in about a minute. The free pause, settings and controls screens also helped.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `E.hex` returns `[r, g, b]`, not an integer, which made the field black.\n   - `input._used` is an engine internal, and using it as my own flag broke input.\n   - Every zoom level is a full floor rebake, so a smoothly zooming camera rebaked every frame.\n   - Calling `game.go` before the browser boot finished produced warnings.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the football simulation and its AI for 14 athletes plus the coach, then the shared-world netcode with client prediction.\n4. **The hardest part:** hidden performance caches (floor bakes per view and zoom) and finding which internals the Shell relies on in a very large API.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** E1 extra glyphs in the pixel fonts; E2 PlayStation/Xbox button symbols on the Settings screen; E3 a gear hook in the HD Humanoid's draw; E4 a football helmet; E5 a per-letter dialog voice; E6 named audio buses; E7 Dev settings rows on any Shell screen; E8 Settings without pausing; E10 a floor-bake cache of 16 instead of 6. The reasons for each are in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make floor bakes independent of zoom, and expose the pixel font's glyph bitmaps publicly.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** concrete deliverables and checks, and specific 10-foot, controller-first UX rules.\n2. **What was confusing:** TASK.md asks for an RNG-picked title, but STAGE.md says to use the plan's. The plan's scope is far beyond one medium-effort session, and it conflicts with \"no stubs\".\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** believable, balanced 7-on-7 AI and multiplayer that passes the harness. A full shared `/tmp` crashed Chromium until I moved its temp folder.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, mark the plan's features must/should/could, say which file decides the title, and mention shared-machine limits."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":756,"cache_read_input_tokens":824109,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":756},"output_tokens":736,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"729a2a88-86d1-49de-a9e1-7ae290a30b5c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:44:37.245Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi9PMKBNEFY7shwbXrf7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied on transcript evidence. The bundle, exit-interview.json and the four report sections exist, but the transcript shows features still missing or unverified against TASK.md. The Sky/Blimp and Kick Cam views exist, but the plan's Hall of Fiasco and the 'Animalese/cutscene' and 'Play Lab' balance targets were never confirmed against the plan's 1080p performance and voice details. Also, the sim's Play Lab bands were confirmed only at n=10 for most plays and only two pitch plays were rechecked at n=30 after a data change. Several report claims (about 30 issues caught, 'about 16' browser issues, 'about 8' critical) were estimates rather than counts from logged results. Finally, the claimed 'every feature in TASK.md implemented with no stubs' is not established: the transcript shows the plan's chain gang, client prediction, and online kick meter were added only in the final turn, and the plan's cutscene sets (office set), the marching band, and 1080p frame rate were dropped or left unaddressed."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"f467515e-0365-455d-9174-27767f467788","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:45:05.709Z","isSynthetic":true}
