{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiARLk7U3d9JeZBbCCFj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**HAMERICAN FOOTBALL** is finished. The game is `HamericanFootball-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-070556.html` in this folder's root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field answered. The full test run passes (975 of 975), and the AI Hub harness passes all four of its configurations on the final file.\n\nTo show every TASK.md feature is in place, I added a `task` suite to `npm test`; it checks each TASK.md requirement against the built game, and all 30 checks pass:\n- **Package:** one self-contained .html in the folder root; the AI Hub client is the first script; the hub session tags are present; no audio files anywhere (all sound is code); built on my-3D2dge.\n- **Title:** the RNG-drawn \"Hamerican Football\" is on the title screen and the page.\n- **No stubs:** no TODO, FIXME, placeholder or stub in the game sources.\n- **Exit interview:** every Source Information field, 4 + 6 + 4 feedback answers, and the full aiHub block.\n- **About/Credits:** carries all four report sections.\n- **Game content:** 17 offensive plays and 9 defenses, 5 teams plus a practice squad, 8 camera views, the football helmet, logos, the 18 motion clips, gibberish voices that never say the real words, 6 story chapters with cutscenes, and the commentary banks.\n- **Dev mode:** reachable from the pause menu, with live sliders that change play and reset to defaults.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 s idle and exits on any input.\n- **Multiplayer:** a second local player joins, with each half of a shared keyboard getting its own buttons; the AI Hub adapter is present.\n- **Controls:** prompts follow the device (✕ by default, A with the Xbox setting, Space on a keyboard), and menus are big focus lists.\n- **Touch-only devices:** the \"Controller or keyboard required\" gate shows, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Errors:** none in any of it.\n\n**Frame rate:** I did not optimize. TASK.md says \"Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization\". The plan's 1920×1080 item is a look check, and its screenshots read clearly. Measured on an idle machine, the game runs at 45–60 fps at 720p and 45–50 fps at 1080p.\n\n**Issue counts:** the 41 issues are itemized in `ISSUES.md` in the dev folder, each with how it was found and its fix. It is my session log, not test-runner output.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:05:56\n- **Source:** American football (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (title redrawn with a real RNG per TASK.md: Hamerican Football)\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade sports (7-on-7 American football) with beat-'em-up hits and a story campaign. The camera is a sideline broadcast view (custom 50°), with Sky Cam and Blimp options, a Kick Cam, and field-level, isometric and reverse replay angles.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, the mocap runtime with an 18-clip set, Canvas 2D and Web Audio. Tests use Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native HD pixel-art rigs at PS1 resolution, code-painted fields, stands and crowds, and five lit stadiums with weather. New helmets, pads, numbers, logos, card art, four cutscene sets and a marching band.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and E.MOVES, joint post-processing (tilt, roll, lift), and 18 motion-capture clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU).\n- **Audio:** all in code: 11 original songs with layers and stings, 50 sound effects, a crowd bed with chants, and an Animalese/Simlish babble voice synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players. The host runs the world and sends snapshots at 14 Hz; each client predicts its own player and runs its own play-call screen and kick meter. Also local play for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:**\n  - *Allowed:* headless tests with `game.advance` and `game.debug`, a scripted whole-game playthrough, one command that prints only failures and a count, and screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n  - *Used:* `npm test` (15 suites, 975 checks, including the `task` suite). Also the plan's balance bands at n = 30 (all 17 plays inside), Playwright screenshots including the 1080p look check, the engine's check tool, and the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 pass).\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine and AI Hub documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown. No token or cost figures are visible to me. The session ran about 1 h 55 min (07:06 to about 09:00).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 41, each fixed and listed in `ISSUES.md`:\n   - **Headless suites (15):** for example, a new match replaying the previous match's play calls, a crash on a kick play, and the touch-only gate unlocking itself.\n   - **Browser runs and screenshots (17):** for example, three crashes and a syntax error, a black field, 12 fps from field rebakes, and characters the pixel font lacks drawn as \"?\".\n   - **Play Lab and the plan's bands (9):** for example, runs averaging 13 yards, a pass rush that sacked in 0.8 s, and blocks that never broke off.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 9:\n   - the boot crash, the input crash and the update crash;\n   - the syntax error that stopped the page loading;\n   - the black field;\n   - the stale play calls;\n   - the kick-play crash;\n   - 12 fps rendering;\n   - the 0.8 s pass rush.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the rules suite's back-to-back scenario runs, which exposed the stale play calls. The scripted story playthrough was the best broad net.\n4. **Least helpful test:** my first spin test, which could never fail until I rewrote it, and the check tool's stock camera views.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the HD Humanoid with mocap, and exact headless `game.advance`. The whole suite and a full playthrough run in under a minute.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `E.hex` returns `[r, g, b]`, not an integer.\n   - `input._used` is an engine internal, and using it broke input.\n   - Every zoom level is a full floor rebake.\n   - Calling `game.go` before the browser boot finished caused warnings.\n   - The pixel font silently draws \"?\" for characters it lacks.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the football simulation and its AI for 14 athletes and the coach, then the shared-world netcode with prediction.\n4. **The hardest part:** hidden caches (floor bakes per view and zoom) and a very large API.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n\n   | # | Change | Why |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | E1 | Glyphs added to both pixel fonts | PlayStation symbols drew as \"?\" |\n   | E2 | PlayStation/Xbox button symbols on Settings | TASK.md asks for PS glyphs with an Xbox option |\n   | E3 | A gear hook in the HD Humanoid's draw | Pads, numbers and the carried ball inside the rig |\n   | E4 | A football helmet hat style | A helmet that reads as football |\n   | E5 | A per-letter dialog voice | The Animalese voices |\n   | E6 | Named audio buses | Separate voice and crowd volumes |\n   | E7 | Dev settings rows on any Shell screen | Dev mode's live tuning sliders |\n   | E8 | Settings without pausing | AI Hub sessions must not freeze |\n   | E10 | A floor-bake cache of 16 instead of 6 | The field was rebaking every frame |\n\n   Details are in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** zoom-independent floor bakes, public access to the font's glyph bitmaps, and a warning when the font has no glyph for a character.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** concrete deliverables and checks, and specific 10-foot, controller-first UX rules.\n2. **What was confusing:** TASK.md wants an RNG-picked title, while STAGE.md says to use the plan's. The plan's scope also far exceeds one medium-effort session, which conflicts with \"no stubs\".\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** believable, balanced 7-on-7 AI, and multiplayer that passes the harness. A full shared `/tmp` also crashed Chromium until I moved its temp folder.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, mark the plan's features must/should/could, say which file decides the title, and note shared-machine limits."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":544,"cache_read_input_tokens":876855,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":544},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49428dbe-779b-4248-8ac3-c582018d3c3d","uuid":"0ee85104-02d7-424e-8261-9936a9a41115","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:58:10.210Z","request_id":"req_011CgiARLJ5riaFsC7qtYq1L"}
